1
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:50,490
What is it? Hospital waste?

2
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:52,960
Don't you ever give up?

3
00:01:46,650 --> 00:01:47,880
Pauline.

4
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:51,770
What is it, babe?

5
00:01:52,210 --> 00:01:55,010
What, is it Henri? Has something happened?

6
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:57,370
Paul.

7
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:00,930
I can't go on any more.
I just want to get away.

8
00:02:01,010 --> 00:02:04,160
We can't. We're almost to court now.

9
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:09,530
- I just don't think I can face it.
- We got the evidence.

10
00:02:09,770 --> 00:02:13,610
Just hold on for Henri. Come on.
You can do it.

11
00:02:29,410 --> 00:02:30,850
Did the Attorney General come back

12
00:02:30,930 --> 00:02:34,010
about the Home Secretary's alleged corruption yet?

13
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:36,050
Do you think you should keep pressing that, Judge?

14
00:02:36,055 --> 00:02:38,655
It's going to make people very angry.

15
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:42,200
There's no going back now.
Call his secretary again.

16
00:02:53,450 --> 00:02:55,440
Did my secretary organise coffee?

17
00:02:55,530 --> 00:02:59,970
I think we need something a little
stronger if we're to get through this.

18
00:03:04,170 --> 00:03:07,320
If you're earnest about impeaching Deed
before Parliament rises,

19
00:03:07,410 --> 00:03:08,890
we have to get moving.

20
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:12,360
Is the Lord Chancellor really
going ahead with this, Ian?

21
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:15,890
- There's little choice now.
- Deed could always resign.

22
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:18,090
He won't though, Joe.

23
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:21,370
Hardly. He's been hounding
me for a judicial enquiry

24
00:03:21,450 --> 00:03:24,840
into Haughton's relation with Listfield.
That would ruin Haughton's career.

25
00:03:24,930 --> 00:03:30,010
I hope we're not pursuing Deed simply
because he's gunning for Haughton.

26
00:03:30,090 --> 00:03:31,810
Of course not, Joe.

27
00:03:31,890 --> 00:03:34,570
Deed admitted sleeping with this claimant.

28
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:38,040
He's just trying to slip off the hook
by going after the Home Secretary.

29
00:03:38,130 --> 00:03:40,570
Some of the Cabinet are asking
why he can't be stopped.

30
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:44,810
Well, he can't, unless you want to strip
all judges of all their powers.

31
00:03:44,890 --> 00:03:47,810
At present, you have the support of the judges.

32
00:03:47,890 --> 00:03:50,090
But you won't if you interfere
with their integrity.

33
00:03:50,170 --> 00:03:51,240
No.

34
00:03:51,330 --> 00:03:57,120
I mean, if Deed does have evidence of Haughton's
behaving corruptly

35
00:03:57,210 --> 00:04:01,130
then you try to impeach him, it will look
like a cover-up.

36
00:04:02,810 --> 00:04:06,090
You don't think Deed has
information that we don't?

37
00:04:09,770 --> 00:04:14,290
If that's the case, I'd better go and see him
and find out what he has got.

38
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:19,560
Yeah, but is there evidence that Haughton
accepted a bribe?

39
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:23,440
Only Rufus Barron could tell us that
and he won't talk to me.

40
00:04:24,370 --> 00:04:26,970
Oh, it's so dangerous to pursue this.

41
00:04:27,410 --> 00:04:30,770
The accusation against Haughton as got to
be investigated.

42
00:04:30,850 --> 00:04:33,290
- What are we...
- You're on shaky ground.

43
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:35,650
The gossip around the Temple is

44
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:39,490
that they're going to impeach you
for sleeping with the claimant.

45
00:04:39,570 --> 00:04:40,610
That's just a smokescreen.

46
00:04:40,690 --> 00:04:43,290
Judges have done far worse things
than sleep with a claimant.

47
00:04:43,370 --> 00:04:45,650
Yes, maybe, but the end result is the same.

48
00:04:45,730 --> 00:04:48,010
You lose your job or worse.

49
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:51,010
Jo, I don't care.

50
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:55,640
John, they threatened to kill me, and they
threatened Michael.

51
00:04:55,730 --> 00:04:58,690
Which is exactly why they have to be stopped.

52
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:05,250
Are you still hearing the claim against Cardinal?

53
00:05:05,255 --> 00:05:08,930
- You heard something different?
- My clerk said there might be a delay.

54
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:10,770
They're trying to get the case away from you.

55
00:05:10,775 --> 00:05:13,295
They won't. Are you ready?

56
00:05:15,530 --> 00:05:17,530
John, a lot of people are counting on this action.

57
00:05:17,535 --> 00:05:20,735
I do not want this to become
a political football.

58
00:05:21,130 --> 00:05:23,890
With what's at stake, that's
exactly what's going to happen.

59
00:05:23,970 --> 00:05:27,170
Jo, listen. I can handle this.

60
00:05:35,170 --> 00:05:37,850
- Sir Michael Niven, judge.
- Thanks, Coop.

61
00:05:39,450 --> 00:05:43,890
- This is not altogether unexpected.
- We all have your interests at heart.

62
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:48,730
Unpleasant as it is, I...

63
00:05:48,810 --> 00:05:52,090
I am required to remove the
Cardinal case from you.

64
00:05:52,810 --> 00:05:55,090
Will you arm wrestle me for it?

65
00:05:56,690 --> 00:06:00,160
Keeping a case you've started,
it's only a convention.

66
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:07,610
Mrs Mills is before you, along with your daughter.

67
00:06:08,490 --> 00:06:11,130
Isn't that cause enough to give it up?

68
00:06:11,570 --> 00:06:15,360
- Where's this coming from?
- You shouldn't be hearing cases.

69
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:19,210
Oh, no. Who sent you, Michael?

70
00:06:19,290 --> 00:06:20,770
Ian Rochester?

71
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:23,770
Or the Home Secretary?

72
00:06:25,210 --> 00:06:28,050
You see, all this just convinces
me of to give ground.

73
00:06:28,130 --> 00:06:32,210
Regrettably, John, you behaved
improperly with a claimant.

74
00:06:32,290 --> 00:06:33,690
I urge you.

75
00:06:36,370 --> 00:06:39,810
No. I beg you as a friend.

76
00:06:40,610 --> 00:06:41,840
Step back.

77
00:06:41,930 --> 00:06:44,240
Not while the dead hand of the Home Secretary

78
00:06:44,330 --> 00:06:46,370
lies across the judiciary.

79
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:48,520
I won't.

80
00:06:50,090 --> 00:06:54,170
Then there's little point
in sitting through a disagreeable lunch.

81
00:07:01,010 --> 00:07:05,090
It won't end here.
Michael Niven was supposed to succeed.

82
00:07:05,610 --> 00:07:07,680
He's a political apparatchik.

83
00:07:07,770 --> 00:07:10,490
What we're losing here is separation of powers.

84
00:07:10,570 --> 00:07:14,170
Possibly the fuss you're making
about Haughton isn't helping.

85
00:07:14,250 --> 00:07:17,130
What's that go to do with the Cardinal case?

86
00:07:19,490 --> 00:07:24,520
I think perhaps Tim Listfield is in some way
connected to Cardinal.

87
00:07:27,490 --> 00:07:29,880
Well, Deed will be totally biased.

88
00:07:29,970 --> 00:07:32,770
I'm not sure if it's wise for
me to be further involved.

89
00:07:32,850 --> 00:07:34,490
But he has to be stopped.

90
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:36,290
George, raise the question of his removal.

91
00:07:36,370 --> 00:07:38,130
That's a valid reason for him not sitting.

92
00:07:38,210 --> 00:07:41,920
- Will it happen?
- And fast. The man's out of control.

93
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:44,170
Let's stop him.

94
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:51,570
With the utmost respect, my lord,
should you be hearing this case?

95
00:07:53,970 --> 00:07:57,120
- Your argument is?
- Because of your impeachment.

96
00:07:57,210 --> 00:07:59,970
- Is that still happening?
- I understand it is.

97
00:08:00,050 --> 00:08:02,520
Then you understand more than I do.

98
00:08:03,850 --> 00:08:06,000
In the circumstances, mightn't it be better

99
00:08:06,090 --> 00:08:08,210
to allow the case to go before another judge?

100
00:08:08,290 --> 00:08:12,130
Even if I were of a mind to do so,
you're too late. Mrs Mills.

101
00:08:16,250 --> 00:08:18,160
In the report we submitted,

102
00:08:18,250 --> 00:08:22,330
following the further examination
of the plant by Professor Moriarty,

103
00:08:22,410 --> 00:08:24,850
there were dioxins found in
far higher concentrations

104
00:08:24,930 --> 00:08:26,470
than those currently permitted.

105
00:08:26,671 --> 00:08:29,874
It would be unfair to the
defendants were we to proceed.

106
00:08:30,075 --> 00:08:32,100
If you don't, I'll find for the claimants.

107
00:08:32,190 --> 00:08:34,630
Yes, of course. You would.

108
00:08:35,630 --> 00:08:38,590
Yes, of course.
It's an appropriate response.

109
00:08:39,550 --> 00:08:43,070
I would like an adjournment of 28 days to prepare
the defence in the light

110
00:08:43,150 --> 00:08:46,070
of your hearing this.
- No, Mrs Channing. Are you ready?

111
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:49,940
Then I would like a short adjournment to consult
Sir Peter Tufton, my client.

112
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:53,500
Oh, very well.
Let my clerk know when you're ready.

113
00:08:53,590 --> 00:08:55,190
All rise.

114
00:09:03,830 --> 00:09:05,820
This is how it'll be.

115
00:09:05,910 --> 00:09:08,220
Can't we just get the case
in front of another judge?

116
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:11,590
Not unless you have a plan, Peter.
I don't.

117
00:09:12,470 --> 00:09:14,780
Am I going to have to stop Deed?

118
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:18,470
Tim, the more he pushes for his judicial enquiry,

119
00:09:18,550 --> 00:09:20,780
the tighter his noose becomes.

120
00:09:21,430 --> 00:09:24,150
Good. What about Cardinal?

121
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:28,390
Oh, Deed will be off the bench
long before that reaches judgment.

122
00:09:31,150 --> 00:09:35,750
Ten years ago, Mrs Peacock inherited
Rosebank Cottage from her grandfather,

123
00:09:35,830 --> 00:09:39,540
a pretty little country idyll
where she tried to raise a family.

124
00:09:39,630 --> 00:09:41,990
That's when their troubles began,

125
00:09:42,070 --> 00:09:45,540
after the cottage became overshadowed
by a waste incinerator plant.

126
00:09:45,630 --> 00:09:49,100
Which the council had endless
public consultations about.

127
00:09:50,390 --> 00:09:53,230
Local people felt their voice wasn't heard.

128
00:09:53,750 --> 00:09:56,140
Presumably their rubbish was being dealt with?

129
00:09:56,230 --> 00:10:00,910
Well, Cardinal are taking rubbish from
afar away as Essex and Southampton,

130
00:10:00,990 --> 00:10:03,430
South London and the French coast.

131
00:10:03,630 --> 00:10:05,940
As other communities have found to their cost,

132
00:10:06,030 --> 00:10:08,710
the incinerator has resulted
in life-threatening dioxins

133
00:10:08,790 --> 00:10:10,270
being released into the atmosphere...

134
00:10:10,275 --> 00:10:13,390
My lord, I cannot suffer these
injustices to my client.

135
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:15,830
I'm sure you know how these cases work.

136
00:10:15,910 --> 00:10:20,860
- First the claimant, then the defendant.
- Then you decide in her favour.

137
00:10:20,950 --> 00:10:24,580
Only if she can prove a causal
link between the incinerator

138
00:10:24,670 --> 00:10:26,710
and the health of the Peacocks.
- Mrs Mills.

139
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:28,780
We will show such a link.

140
00:10:30,670 --> 00:10:35,190
In 1998, soon after the
incinerator was up and running,

141
00:10:35,270 --> 00:10:39,030
Mrs Peacock started to get flu-like symptoms.

142
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:41,790
It was winter, so she assumed it was flu.

143
00:10:41,870 --> 00:10:46,390
But they persisted and then
her husband developed the same symptoms.

144
00:10:46,750 --> 00:10:48,990
Finally, his doctor suggested
that he might be suffering

145
00:10:48,995 --> 00:10:52,150
from chemical poisoning from handling
treated wood. He's a carpenter.

146
00:10:52,230 --> 00:10:57,150
Mrs Peacock being contaminated by contact
with her husband's clothes.

147
00:10:57,230 --> 00:11:00,430
This persisted until in 2001,

148
00:11:00,510 --> 00:11:02,740
Mrs Peacock gave birth to their first child,

149
00:11:02,830 --> 00:11:06,590
a boy, who lived only hours.
- His heart and lungs were fused.

150
00:11:08,230 --> 00:11:11,270
Around the same time, another mother in the area

151
00:11:11,350 --> 00:11:14,580
gave birth to a baby with teratogenic deformities.

152
00:11:15,270 --> 00:11:18,630
Several other patients at
the same medical practice

153
00:11:18,710 --> 00:11:20,620
as the one that Mrs Peacock attends

154
00:11:20,710 --> 00:11:24,670
were also misdiagnosed with these mysterious
flu-like symptoms.

155
00:11:25,150 --> 00:11:29,830
Are you implying doctors colluded
with Cardinal to disguise these facts?

156
00:11:30,310 --> 00:11:32,550
I'm saying they didn't know
what they were talking about

157
00:11:32,555 --> 00:11:35,075
when they blamed factors other than
chlorinated hydrocarbons,

158
00:11:35,155 --> 00:11:38,110
that is to say, dioxins
produced by the incinerator.

159
00:11:38,830 --> 00:11:41,220
Sir Alan Peasmarsh's secretary rang.

160
00:11:41,310 --> 00:11:44,030
She asked if you could meet with Sir Alan today.

161
00:11:44,110 --> 00:11:47,580
- The earliest I said you could is 6:00.
- Fine.

162
00:11:53,230 --> 00:11:54,630
Thanks, Jo.

163
00:11:59,550 --> 00:12:01,510
- John.
- Hello, Neil.

164
00:12:01,590 --> 00:12:03,870
You could talk to me, John.

165
00:12:03,950 --> 00:12:06,750
What would you say?
- That the allegation's not true?

166
00:12:06,830 --> 00:12:09,550
- It's not.
- Oh. Pleased to hear it.

167
00:12:11,870 --> 00:12:15,390
You have the Director of Public Prosecutions
at 6:20, Sir Alan.

168
00:12:15,470 --> 00:12:18,510
- So I do. Warn me at 6:15, will you?
- Yes, certainly.

169
00:12:19,390 --> 00:12:20,510
John.

170
00:12:24,390 --> 00:12:29,590
Is what's going on between you.
- and Neil Haughton personal?

171
00:12:30,910 --> 00:12:33,750
The allegations against him emerged in open court

172
00:12:33,830 --> 00:12:36,390
as part of Rufus Barron's defence.

173
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:38,590
A judicial enquiry would ruin his career.

174
00:12:38,670 --> 00:12:41,950
- Impeachment would ruin mine.
- Well, the two aren't connected.

175
00:12:42,030 --> 00:12:43,990
That's very reassuring, Alan,

176
00:12:44,070 --> 00:12:46,870
as Haughton is head of the
criminal justice system.

177
00:12:46,950 --> 00:12:51,110
Do you have any evidence that
Haughton behaved improperly?

178
00:12:51,190 --> 00:12:54,420
Tim Listfield's company was
awarded a defence contract

179
00:12:54,510 --> 00:12:58,470
that went 700 million pounds
over budget on Haughton's watch.

180
00:12:58,550 --> 00:13:02,910
- That alone warrants an enquiry.
- Well, contracts are often over budget.

181
00:13:02,990 --> 00:13:04,470
So has it not occurred to anybody

182
00:13:04,550 --> 00:13:06,910
that double and triple billing might be endemic?

183
00:13:06,990 --> 00:13:10,300
- It's not a precise science.
- It's taxpayers' money!

184
00:13:10,390 --> 00:13:12,830
But you need the evidence, John.

185
00:13:13,430 --> 00:13:16,550
So I will go and talk to Rufus Barron again.

186
00:13:18,550 --> 00:13:20,860
I think you'll be ill-advised to do that.

187
00:13:20,950 --> 00:13:25,390
There are those in the Cabinet who
feel that this is a personal vendetta.

188
00:13:26,470 --> 00:13:28,430
Then we have an impasse.

189
00:13:30,430 --> 00:13:34,510
I'll get the police to interview Barron.
- That's the best I can do.

190
00:13:40,310 --> 00:13:44,910
Right, her feed's in the fridge, Mum.
- Can you try and get her to eat it all?

191
00:13:45,310 --> 00:13:48,990
- New nappies are in the cupboard.
- Pauline, I have done this before.

192
00:13:49,070 --> 00:13:51,110
I know. It's just nerves.

193
00:14:11,950 --> 00:14:15,580
Mrs Peacock, is Rosebank Cottage
a healthy place to live?

194
00:14:15,670 --> 00:14:18,820
Erm, it was when I was a girl.

195
00:14:19,950 --> 00:14:21,910
Gran and Grandad grew all their own fruit and veg

196
00:14:21,990 --> 00:14:25,430
and got water from their well.
- Do you still use the well?

197
00:14:25,510 --> 00:14:26,550
No.

198
00:14:27,470 --> 00:14:32,390
After I got pregnant, the council tested the water
and condemned it.

199
00:14:32,470 --> 00:14:36,100
Was this before or after the
incinerator plant was running?

200
00:14:36,190 --> 00:14:38,710
About three years after it started.

201
00:14:39,230 --> 00:14:42,590
I'd been having a lot of difficulty.
- getting pregnant.

202
00:14:43,470 --> 00:14:45,430
And then I had a spontaneous abortion.

203
00:14:45,510 --> 00:14:49,350
Was anyone else prone to spontaneous
abortions in your family?

204
00:14:51,470 --> 00:14:55,790
- No one's ever had any sort of abortion.
- Did you find an explanation?

205
00:14:56,550 --> 00:14:59,510
My doctor just said it happens to some women.

206
00:15:01,230 --> 00:15:04,590
Can you tell us about the
birth of your first child?

207
00:15:04,870 --> 00:15:09,110
Josh was born at 24 weeks.

208
00:15:09,630 --> 00:15:12,390
He only lived long enough to be baptised.

209
00:15:12,470 --> 00:15:14,860
His heart and lungs were joined together.

210
00:15:14,950 --> 00:15:17,420
- Was any explanation given?
- No.

211
00:15:18,430 --> 00:15:21,430
The hospital said I should just
try and have another baby.

212
00:15:21,510 --> 00:15:25,550
- And did you?
- Yes. Henri, she's 18 months.

213
00:15:25,630 --> 00:15:27,230
Is Henri normal?

214
00:15:30,150 --> 00:15:31,470
She has...

215
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:35,940
She has spina bifida.

216
00:15:40,110 --> 00:15:44,430
She's only got one eye and no nose.

217
00:15:48,870 --> 00:15:52,230
Mrs Peacock, if this is too upsetting for you,
we can take a break.

218
00:15:52,310 --> 00:15:54,510
No. It's all right.

219
00:15:55,750 --> 00:15:57,150
It's just...

220
00:15:58,750 --> 00:16:03,270
People never want to look at our baby.
- You see them looking away.

221
00:16:06,870 --> 00:16:09,590
I just want them to say something nice.

222
00:16:10,670 --> 00:16:13,900
She's ever so cheerful. She is.

223
00:16:17,510 --> 00:16:20,710
- Do you get any help with Henri?
- My mum helps.

224
00:16:21,910 --> 00:16:25,220
But, at times, I think she
wishes she hadn't lived.

225
00:16:25,750 --> 00:16:28,310
Henri needs constant attention.

226
00:16:28,950 --> 00:16:31,750
The council said.
- I don't qualify for help.

227
00:16:32,230 --> 00:16:35,270
At times, I get so tired.
- I think I can't go on.

228
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:41,270
But then you see her trying to
do things like other children.

229
00:16:43,390 --> 00:16:46,110
Did anyone ever suggest that Henri's problems

230
00:16:46,190 --> 00:16:48,750
were caused by the waste incinerator plant?

231
00:16:48,830 --> 00:16:51,190
That's the last thing anyone wanted.

232
00:16:51,270 --> 00:16:54,870
They blamed Paul working with
chemically treated wood and chipboard.

233
00:16:54,950 --> 00:16:57,230
Did you discover the real source?

234
00:16:57,310 --> 00:17:00,670
My lord, does my learned friend know the truth?

235
00:17:00,750 --> 00:17:02,710
Isn't that what this hearing is for?

236
00:17:02,790 --> 00:17:06,390
Mrs Mills, it would be better
if you didn't draw conclusions.

237
00:17:06,470 --> 00:17:08,780
Did you try to get to the truth?

238
00:17:09,310 --> 00:17:12,860
After what happened to Henri,
we started searching the Internet.

239
00:17:12,950 --> 00:17:16,180
A pile of stuff came up about dioxins being

240
00:17:16,270 --> 00:17:19,580
carcinogenic, teratogenic and mutagenic.

241
00:17:19,670 --> 00:17:24,430
They're in pesticides and things,
but they also come from incinerators

242
00:17:24,510 --> 00:17:30,070
where they burn chlorinated rubbish like
paper and packaging and hospital stuff.

243
00:17:30,790 --> 00:17:32,430
And what did you do next?

244
00:17:32,510 --> 00:17:36,110
We went to the council and to the incinerator.

245
00:17:36,590 --> 00:17:39,110
They just gave us the brush off.

246
00:17:39,190 --> 00:17:43,190
Then my GP got keen on the dioxins angle
and started investigating.

247
00:17:43,270 --> 00:17:47,310
But then suddenly she changed tack
and said my problem was hereditary.

248
00:17:47,390 --> 00:17:49,300
I wouldn't accept that.

249
00:17:49,390 --> 00:17:54,100
My new doctor went through my history
and couldn't find any hereditary cause.

250
00:17:54,190 --> 00:17:58,510
What happened to your first GP?
- Um, Dr Matthews, I believe.

251
00:17:59,230 --> 00:18:01,590
She got a grant to do epidemiology.

252
00:18:01,670 --> 00:18:06,270
That's studying the cause of disease
in the community at Sussex University.

253
00:18:07,030 --> 00:18:09,870
Thank you. Was this funded
by the health authority?

254
00:18:09,950 --> 00:18:13,230
I don't know. I mean,
- I think it was part of Cardinal's

255
00:18:13,310 --> 00:18:16,780
dirty tricks campaign when
we started a public protest.

256
00:18:17,270 --> 00:18:21,310
Do you have evidence that Cardinal
did anything at all underhand?

257
00:18:22,870 --> 00:18:25,230
Well, no, not really.

258
00:18:29,870 --> 00:18:33,830
Did your new GP find any other patients
who suffered similarly?

259
00:18:33,910 --> 00:18:36,510
A boy with problems like Henri.

260
00:18:37,105 --> 00:18:38,825
They thought that pesticides were to blame.

261
00:18:38,830 --> 00:18:42,110
His parents live next door to a farmer's field
that was sprayed a lot.

262
00:18:42,190 --> 00:18:44,260
Where did this person live?

263
00:18:45,150 --> 00:18:49,150
- Two miles from where I live.
- Within the incinerator danger zone?

264
00:18:49,230 --> 00:18:50,380
My lord...

265
00:18:50,470 --> 00:18:54,150
Mrs Mills, you've yet to establish
that such a zone exists.

266
00:18:56,590 --> 00:18:58,630
Did your GP see any connection

267
00:18:58,710 --> 00:19:01,230
between the birth defects
and Peaks Farm incinerator?

268
00:19:01,310 --> 00:19:03,350
No, no, no. Mrs Mills, no.

269
00:19:08,790 --> 00:19:12,500
Did your GP find any other similar birth problems?

270
00:19:12,790 --> 00:19:16,580
Well, he started asking other GPS in a
10-mile radius, but he got into trouble.

271
00:19:16,670 --> 00:19:17,990
Who with?

272
00:19:18,070 --> 00:19:22,270
The health authority said that GPS shouldn't be
doing that sort of work.

273
00:19:22,350 --> 00:19:24,070
It wasn't ethical.

274
00:19:24,830 --> 00:19:26,900
Did he find other patients?

275
00:19:28,470 --> 00:19:30,270
About 12.

276
00:19:31,350 --> 00:19:35,350
Twelve within the 10-mile radius
of Peaks Farm incinerator?

277
00:19:36,310 --> 00:19:39,310
The epidemiologist who my GP got to look into it

278
00:19:39,390 --> 00:19:42,390
said that it was too high to be a coincidence.

279
00:19:43,070 --> 00:19:48,470
The norm is 1 birth defect in a 25-mile radius.

280
00:19:52,150 --> 00:19:56,470
Mrs Peacock, you stated there's no
history of abortions in your family.

281
00:19:56,550 --> 00:19:58,380
Your mother had three.

282
00:20:00,270 --> 00:20:01,310
Oh!

283
00:20:02,950 --> 00:20:06,550
- Did... Did she?
- Well, according to her medical records.

284
00:20:06,630 --> 00:20:09,590
My lord, these have not been disclosed to us.

285
00:20:11,150 --> 00:20:13,110
You have an explanation?

286
00:20:16,470 --> 00:20:19,830
I see. I will disregard this evidence.

287
00:20:22,150 --> 00:20:25,270
Despite you and your husband's campaigning,

288
00:20:25,350 --> 00:20:27,740
you're not very well informed.

289
00:20:27,830 --> 00:20:32,540
For example, did you know that dioxins exist
everywhere in the world?

290
00:20:32,870 --> 00:20:35,910
In water, the air, soil.

291
00:20:36,350 --> 00:20:40,430
Yes. But they're heaviest
around waste incinerators.

292
00:20:40,990 --> 00:20:44,110
- Do you sleep in a bed?
- Yes, of course.

293
00:20:44,550 --> 00:20:49,150
- Did you buy your mattress new?
- When we moved into the cottage.

294
00:20:49,550 --> 00:20:54,420
Were you aware that some mattresses
and sheets are laced with chemicals

295
00:20:54,510 --> 00:20:56,980
which can damage the immune system,

296
00:20:57,070 --> 00:21:00,300
cause birth defects, arrest brain development?

297
00:21:02,270 --> 00:21:03,550
Well, no.

298
00:21:04,110 --> 00:21:05,670
Isn't it true, Mrs Peacock,

299
00:21:05,750 --> 00:21:10,110
that through your campaigning
you're more interested in blaming

300
00:21:10,190 --> 00:21:13,420
rather than rectifying the problem or moving on?

301
00:21:13,790 --> 00:21:16,260
That's not true. It's not.

302
00:21:17,150 --> 00:21:20,620
Isn't the problem a little closer to home

303
00:21:20,710 --> 00:21:24,150
with what your husband brought
home from work on his clothes?

304
00:21:24,230 --> 00:21:27,270
- No. We looked into that.
- Did you?

305
00:21:28,070 --> 00:21:31,030
Did you consider it before the
incinerator plant was built

306
00:21:31,110 --> 00:21:33,990
when, in all probability,
the damage was already done

307
00:21:34,070 --> 00:21:37,350
to you and your baby?
- No. It couldn't have.

308
00:21:41,230 --> 00:21:44,670
- John shouldn't be hearing this.
- Yes. I'm sure you're right.

309
00:21:44,750 --> 00:21:47,430
Dad'll probably make it harder for us,
not easier, Mum.

310
00:21:47,510 --> 00:21:49,230
Oh, yes, Charlie.

311
00:21:50,070 --> 00:21:53,540
Grandad's coming for dinner.
- Why don't you join us?

312
00:21:54,590 --> 00:21:56,190
I can't tonight.

313
00:21:57,910 --> 00:22:00,350
Charlie, the Judge would like to see you.

314
00:22:00,430 --> 00:22:02,260
Me too, Coop?

315
00:22:02,350 --> 00:22:03,790
Just Charlie.

316
00:22:08,750 --> 00:22:10,660
Paul, I've got the car.

317
00:22:13,830 --> 00:22:15,710
I never knew that about Mum.

318
00:22:16,910 --> 00:22:19,790
You know their tricks.
- We'll get them back.

319
00:22:21,150 --> 00:22:23,220
That's all you think about.

320
00:22:24,110 --> 00:22:25,710
I'm tired, Paul.

321
00:22:26,910 --> 00:22:28,590
I'm just so weary.

322
00:22:36,390 --> 00:22:39,620
Don't you find it odd, that GP, Dr Matthews,

323
00:22:39,710 --> 00:22:41,910
conveniently going to Sussex?

324
00:22:44,110 --> 00:22:47,470
- Did you find out who funded her?
- I'm not sure.

325
00:22:49,510 --> 00:22:52,980
Well, it is curious.
- I'd have researched that.

326
00:22:54,230 --> 00:22:56,670
Dad, Mum said you shouldn't
even be hearing this case.

327
00:22:56,750 --> 00:22:59,190
Oh, she's fighting Neil's corner.

328
00:23:01,270 --> 00:23:05,230
Do you ever hear from that
lecturer you were close to at Sussex?

329
00:23:05,870 --> 00:23:09,020
- Henry?
- Henry. Yeah. He could be helpful.

330
00:23:09,630 --> 00:23:13,710
- You're supposed to be impartial.
- I'm trying to get to the truth.

331
00:23:16,870 --> 00:23:18,780
I could call Henry.

332
00:23:19,550 --> 00:23:21,860
- I'd have to tell Jo.
- Mmm-hmm.

333
00:23:22,470 --> 00:23:26,470
Best not get back to your Mum, though.
- She could get a retrial.

334
00:23:29,510 --> 00:23:31,230
Do you want dinner?

335
00:23:32,270 --> 00:23:33,990
I can't. I'm sorry.

336
00:23:44,630 --> 00:23:46,860
- Neil's joining us, Daddy.
- What?

337
00:23:46,950 --> 00:23:50,660
- I hope you don't mind.
- I accept that things will change

338
00:23:50,750 --> 00:23:53,030
the more you become part of Haughton's life.

339
00:23:53,110 --> 00:23:55,550
But I don't want to become
part of Haughton's life.

340
00:23:55,630 --> 00:24:00,020
It's these wretched accusations.
- People can't believe them.

341
00:24:00,110 --> 00:24:02,710
People tend to believe what they want to believe.

342
00:24:02,790 --> 00:24:05,510
I won't hear a word said against him.

343
00:24:05,590 --> 00:24:08,790
He should submit to an enquiry to clear his name.

344
00:24:08,870 --> 00:24:10,910
He doesn't see why he should.

345
00:24:12,190 --> 00:24:13,950
Ah, that'll be him.

346
00:24:14,750 --> 00:24:16,230
I think I'll dine at the club.

347
00:24:18,230 --> 00:24:20,670
Joe, how nice. Darling.

348
00:24:20,750 --> 00:24:25,220
- Daddy says he can't stay.
- Oh. I wanted your advice.

349
00:24:25,310 --> 00:24:27,590
Would you take it if I gave it?

350
00:24:29,550 --> 00:24:30,870
Goodnight.

351
00:24:35,910 --> 00:24:37,110
George?

352
00:24:38,470 --> 00:24:42,310
Daddy says you should submit
to an enquiry to clear your name.

353
00:24:42,910 --> 00:24:47,270
- Is that what you want?
- I want whatever makes you happy.

354
00:24:48,310 --> 00:24:51,990
Seeing your ex kicked off the bench will do.

355
00:24:56,950 --> 00:24:58,270
With your leave, my lord,

356
00:24:58,350 --> 00:25:02,390
I'd like to call the epidemiologist,
Professor Margaret Moriarty.

357
00:25:03,790 --> 00:25:08,150
Until recently did you hold the chair in
Community Medicine at Sussex University?

358
00:25:08,230 --> 00:25:11,310
Yes, I did.
- My specialism was in dioxins.

359
00:25:12,270 --> 00:25:15,190
The effects of chemical pollutants
is my main field.

360
00:25:15,270 --> 00:25:18,580
Can you tell us why you're currently
between chairs?

361
00:25:19,030 --> 00:25:22,990
I helped some students involved
in a big pesticide-poisoning case

362
00:25:23,070 --> 00:25:24,830
with their evidence.

363
00:25:24,910 --> 00:25:27,750
The manufacturers took exception to my evidence.

364
00:25:27,830 --> 00:25:30,270
They subsequently made huge funding grants

365
00:25:30,350 --> 00:25:34,590
to the Chemistry and Environmental
Health Departments at the university.

366
00:25:34,670 --> 00:25:36,790
I suspect my contract not being renewed...

367
00:25:36,870 --> 00:25:40,070
My lord, does this have anything to do with
Cardinal Waste?

368
00:25:40,150 --> 00:25:42,030
Quite. Does it, Mrs Mills?

369
00:25:42,110 --> 00:25:45,470
I haven't found a connection between
the funding grant and Cardinal.

370
00:25:45,550 --> 00:25:47,750
- I live in hope.
- Move on.

371
00:25:50,190 --> 00:25:53,230
Professor Moriarty, can you
tell us what dioxins are?

372
00:25:53,310 --> 00:25:56,310
The unintended by-products of industrial processes

373
00:25:56,390 --> 00:25:58,910
which burn chlorine with organic matter.

374
00:25:58,990 --> 00:26:01,030
The principal source?

375
00:26:01,110 --> 00:26:04,630
Incinerating waste,
especially paper waste and plastics.

376
00:26:05,310 --> 00:26:09,100
Incinerated hospital waste is
a major source of dioxins.

377
00:26:09,590 --> 00:26:11,740
They go into the atmosphere?

378
00:26:11,830 --> 00:26:14,870
More than local councils and
incinerator companies tell us.

379
00:26:14,950 --> 00:26:19,030
- Do you have evidence of that?
- Yes. It's mostly kept secret.

380
00:26:19,630 --> 00:26:24,420
Is there a solution to this problem we have with
rubbish in your opinion?

381
00:26:25,270 --> 00:26:29,190
Cut down on the use of chlorinated paper
products and plastics.

382
00:26:29,430 --> 00:26:30,630
I see.

383
00:26:31,830 --> 00:26:36,030
Professor, you recently examined
the incineration at Peaks Farm.

384
00:26:36,110 --> 00:26:37,510
Can you tell us what you found?

385
00:26:37,590 --> 00:26:41,060
Evidence of dioxins in the
chimneys throughout the range.

386
00:26:41,150 --> 00:26:44,940
50% were in the upper range of TEFs, one to four.

387
00:26:45,470 --> 00:26:48,590
- Are they injurious to health?
- Very.

388
00:26:49,830 --> 00:26:51,590
When dioxins enter the food chain

389
00:26:51,670 --> 00:26:55,300
they become embedded in fatty tissue
where they affect our cells.

390
00:26:55,390 --> 00:26:57,300
Pregnant women are most at risk.

391
00:26:57,390 --> 00:27:01,590
Dioxins cause spontaneous abortion,
birth defects, cancer.

392
00:27:01,670 --> 00:27:03,900
They cause cells to divide erroneously,

393
00:27:03,990 --> 00:27:08,310
causing babies to be born with anything
from cleft palates to fused organs.

394
00:27:08,390 --> 00:27:10,300
Chromosome damage occurs

395
00:27:10,390 --> 00:27:14,070
producing gaps and breaks in
both skin and organ tissues.

396
00:27:14,150 --> 00:27:17,230
- Have you seen such birth defects?
- Yes.

397
00:27:18,830 --> 00:27:20,790
They're very disturbing.

398
00:27:22,590 --> 00:27:25,510
Have you read the physicians'
reports on baby Henri?

399
00:27:25,590 --> 00:27:26,990
Yes, I have.

400
00:27:27,350 --> 00:27:31,270
Can you look at the photos in
your bundle marked C at 18?

401
00:27:43,030 --> 00:27:45,310
Is that a photo of baby Henri?

402
00:27:45,670 --> 00:27:47,630
Yes, at about three months.

403
00:27:47,710 --> 00:27:52,260
Does the damage in any way correspond
with the effects of dioxin poisoning?

404
00:27:52,830 --> 00:27:54,660
More or less exactly.

405
00:27:55,390 --> 00:27:58,030
Is there anything you wish to add?

406
00:27:59,590 --> 00:28:02,310
I am puzzled at the different dioxin levels

407
00:28:02,390 --> 00:28:04,830
between what one would expect
from ordinary household waste

408
00:28:04,910 --> 00:28:07,140
and those found at the plant.

409
00:28:07,230 --> 00:28:10,150
They suggest a high rate of PVC burning.

410
00:28:11,030 --> 00:28:14,790
- Is that significant?
- PVC burning indicates hospital waste.

411
00:28:15,110 --> 00:28:17,910
Because this contains far
greater quantities of chlorine,

412
00:28:17,990 --> 00:28:21,140
incinerators are only licensed
to burn small amounts.

413
00:28:21,230 --> 00:28:25,860
Burning chlorine at too low temperatures
causes such high levels of dioxins.

414
00:28:27,110 --> 00:28:28,430
Thank you.

415
00:28:29,070 --> 00:28:31,870
Did you do the principal work in 1987

416
00:28:31,950 --> 00:28:35,420
about sheep dip and the water
tables of England and Wales?

417
00:28:35,510 --> 00:28:39,630
- Yes, I was one of its authors.
- Oh. Is this modesty,

418
00:28:39,710 --> 00:28:42,790
or are you now trying to disassociate.
- yourself from the work?

419
00:28:42,870 --> 00:28:44,350
I'm doing no such thing.

420
00:28:44,430 --> 00:28:46,740
You were, in fact, its main author,

421
00:28:46,830 --> 00:28:49,750
and its conclusions were erroneous, were they not?

422
00:28:49,830 --> 00:28:52,710
- No. They were only contradicted
by chemical companies

423
00:28:52,790 --> 00:28:55,230
whose scientists were bought and paid for.

424
00:28:55,310 --> 00:28:57,910
- Do you have evidence of this?
- It happens all the while.

425
00:28:57,990 --> 00:29:01,990
Big vested interests using the power
of their purse to distort facts.

426
00:29:02,790 --> 00:29:04,780
You don't have evidence.

427
00:29:08,710 --> 00:29:11,180
I see. Thank you.

428
00:29:11,270 --> 00:29:14,310
Your conclusion that pesticides and insecticides

429
00:29:14,390 --> 00:29:16,620
had reached dangerous levels in the water tables

430
00:29:16,710 --> 00:29:20,070
was both wrong and born out
of a political ideology.

431
00:29:20,150 --> 00:29:21,190
No.

432
00:29:22,270 --> 00:29:26,660
Yes. In publishing your paper you
failed to declare the interests

433
00:29:26,750 --> 00:29:29,630
of the environmental group that had funded you.

434
00:29:29,710 --> 00:29:31,910
No. Other organisations were also helping

435
00:29:31,990 --> 00:29:33,820
to fund the chair at the university.

436
00:29:33,910 --> 00:29:37,460
A group that campaigns against what it perceives

437
00:29:37,550 --> 00:29:40,940
to be the big polluters, chemical companies.

438
00:29:41,030 --> 00:29:42,940
My paper wasn't biased.

439
00:29:44,870 --> 00:29:47,310
You were funded by an organisation,

440
00:29:47,390 --> 00:29:49,670
were totally dependent on that organisation,

441
00:29:49,750 --> 00:29:50,910
shared its political outlook,

442
00:29:50,950 --> 00:29:56,310
yet, by some strange quirk of nature,
remained impervious to bias?

443
00:29:57,270 --> 00:29:59,910
- Amazing.
- I wasn't biased.

444
00:30:00,510 --> 00:30:06,070
"Chemical companies conspire to defeat legislation
to prevent them polluting."

445
00:30:06,150 --> 00:30:07,670
Does that sound familiar?

446
00:30:07,750 --> 00:30:10,430
It's true. They don't care about
the future of our children.

447
00:30:10,510 --> 00:30:16,220
These companies get together
and conspire to pollute the planet?

448
00:30:16,310 --> 00:30:21,180
They conspire to maintain profits
by exploiting loopholes in the law.

449
00:30:21,270 --> 00:30:24,550
Then it's fair to say you
come from a biased position.

450
00:30:24,630 --> 00:30:26,350
I'm being factual.

451
00:30:26,750 --> 00:30:30,110
Were you factual when you
accused chemical companies

452
00:30:30,190 --> 00:30:32,550
of forcing you out of your university post?

453
00:30:32,630 --> 00:30:35,470
They did. They made massive grants to get me out.

454
00:30:35,550 --> 00:30:37,510
Not to help further science?

455
00:30:37,590 --> 00:30:40,110
They used all sorts of dirty tricks.

456
00:30:40,190 --> 00:30:42,830
They even sent a psychiatrist
to interview me undercover.

457
00:30:42,910 --> 00:30:45,670
They put out lies about my mental health.

458
00:30:46,550 --> 00:30:50,590
Did you not have to take a leave
of absence due to stress?

459
00:30:50,670 --> 00:30:55,380
Only because of what they were doing,
because I was getting to the truth. I...

460
00:31:00,390 --> 00:31:04,750
If your report on the toxicity
levels at Peaks Farm is true,

461
00:31:05,310 --> 00:31:08,780
why aren't the workers getting ill?
- Some of them are.

462
00:31:08,950 --> 00:31:12,070
They're scared of losing their jobs if they talk.

463
00:31:12,150 --> 00:31:16,700
They might more reasonably fear losing their lives
if what you say is true.

464
00:31:18,390 --> 00:31:20,110
Do you know what Peaks Farm was

465
00:31:20,190 --> 00:31:23,310
before it became a state-of-the-art
incineration facility?

466
00:31:23,390 --> 00:31:25,460
A timber-importing company.

467
00:31:25,830 --> 00:31:30,700
Do chemicals used in the treatment of timber

468
00:31:31,390 --> 00:31:33,110
also cause dioxins?

469
00:31:33,590 --> 00:31:35,660
Yes. I suppose it's possible.

470
00:31:36,030 --> 00:31:38,470
In fact, you know it is.

471
00:31:38,550 --> 00:31:41,430
You wrote a paper in 1996

472
00:31:41,510 --> 00:31:43,790
for your politically active environmental group

473
00:31:43,870 --> 00:31:45,470
condemning the practice, saying it was,

474
00:31:45,550 --> 00:31:48,110
"harming workers in the
industry and their families".

475
00:31:48,190 --> 00:31:49,670
It was.

476
00:31:49,750 --> 00:31:53,270
If Mrs Peacock's baby was affected by dioxins,

477
00:31:53,350 --> 00:31:57,470
it's possible that these came
from the treatment of timber,

478
00:31:57,550 --> 00:31:59,780
not the efficient incinerator.

479
00:32:03,550 --> 00:32:05,190
- Yes?
- No.

480
00:32:05,270 --> 00:32:08,270
Dioxins from their chimneys damaged Henri.

481
00:32:09,350 --> 00:32:13,140
Cardinal are not getting away with it like that.
They're not.

482
00:32:19,670 --> 00:32:21,900
I blew it for you. I'm so sorry.

483
00:32:21,990 --> 00:32:24,220
The judge will assess the facts, not the emotion.

484
00:32:24,310 --> 00:32:27,430
- They just twist everything.
- You made your points.

485
00:32:29,430 --> 00:32:31,070
See you tomorrow.

486
00:32:32,630 --> 00:32:34,390
Did she hurt us, Jo?

487
00:32:55,030 --> 00:32:57,420
Your tutorial was at 5:00.

488
00:32:58,070 --> 00:33:00,510
How would you know if you never
look-up from your screen?

489
00:33:00,590 --> 00:33:01,820
Charlie.

490
00:33:02,510 --> 00:33:05,790
I knew it couldn't be one of my swots.
How are you?

491
00:33:07,070 --> 00:33:09,270
The real world's hard, Henry.

492
00:33:09,590 --> 00:33:12,870
Too many criminals.
- You should have opted for tort.

493
00:33:13,790 --> 00:33:15,620
Something interesting?

494
00:33:15,870 --> 00:33:19,070
Passengers suing a cruise company for rough seas.

495
00:33:22,110 --> 00:33:24,750
- Do you like what you see?
- Oh, Charlie,

496
00:33:24,830 --> 00:33:27,430
still too upfront for a man's ease.

497
00:33:27,510 --> 00:33:29,980
You're going to make a frightening judge.

498
00:33:30,070 --> 00:33:32,060
If I survive long enough.

499
00:33:33,470 --> 00:33:37,100
- Let's go for some coffee.
- What about your 5:00?

500
00:33:37,350 --> 00:33:40,900
Well, probably no more serious
about tort than you were.

501
00:33:41,670 --> 00:33:43,980
I do remember Professor Moriarty.

502
00:33:44,070 --> 00:33:47,070
How could you forget Sherlock Holmes' nemesis?

503
00:33:47,350 --> 00:33:51,350
She seemed to think everyone was conspiring
to get rid of her.

504
00:33:51,430 --> 00:33:53,110
Were they?

505
00:33:53,190 --> 00:33:56,870
I doubt it. There's never enough time
in the academic year.

506
00:33:57,670 --> 00:34:01,140
Soon after her departure, funding did
come through for the new science block.

507
00:34:01,230 --> 00:34:02,990
It was coincidental.

508
00:34:04,030 --> 00:34:07,660
I didn't see any connection.
I was on the committee at the time.

509
00:34:07,750 --> 00:34:10,790
The money gets pledged and
Margaret Moriarty departs

510
00:34:11,270 --> 00:34:13,830
with a very good severance package.

511
00:34:13,910 --> 00:34:16,710
I wish they'd let me go on the same terms.

512
00:34:20,030 --> 00:34:22,100
The money didn't come from the chemical companies.

513
00:34:22,190 --> 00:34:26,820
It was some sort of PR firm.
Mentor, I think they were called.

514
00:34:26,910 --> 00:34:30,380
Why's a PR company funding
science?-It must be millions.

515
00:34:30,470 --> 00:34:33,990
I suppose they could be a conduit
for science-based companies.

516
00:34:34,070 --> 00:34:36,990
Could that include an
incineration plant like Cardinal?

517
00:34:37,070 --> 00:34:38,140
I've no idea.

518
00:34:38,230 --> 00:34:40,390
Is this a court case or another
one of your campaigns?

519
00:34:40,395 --> 00:34:43,355
All I ever have time for is work now, Henry.

520
00:34:44,350 --> 00:34:47,070
What about the GP who came here?
Dr Alison Matthews?

521
00:34:47,150 --> 00:34:49,910
She was studying epidemiology.
- Was she funded by this PR company

522
00:34:49,990 --> 00:34:51,510
to do her research?
- How would I know?

523
00:34:51,515 --> 00:34:53,540
I'm not even on the finance committee now.

524
00:34:53,630 --> 00:34:55,310
This is important, Henry.

525
00:34:55,390 --> 00:34:59,020
Charlie, I don't think I could
even find such information.

526
00:35:02,550 --> 00:35:05,150
All right. Give me your phone number.

527
00:35:09,630 --> 00:35:12,550
Charlie Deed. Hey, Henry.

528
00:35:14,270 --> 00:35:17,420
Well, that's brilliant.
Then there was some sort of conspiracy.

529
00:35:17,510 --> 00:35:18,830
You may think that,

530
00:35:18,910 --> 00:35:22,700
but remember, in law you can have
correlation without causation.

531
00:35:22,790 --> 00:35:25,020
I'm betting there is a causal link.

532
00:35:25,590 --> 00:35:28,430
All right, Henry. Thank you. I'll call you.

533
00:35:31,270 --> 00:35:33,230
An old friend at Sussex.

534
00:35:34,030 --> 00:35:35,630
A PR company called Mentor

535
00:35:35,710 --> 00:35:38,670
sponsored Mrs Peacock's first GP, Dr Matthews,

536
00:35:38,750 --> 00:35:41,270
after she'd made the dioxins connection.

537
00:35:41,350 --> 00:35:43,820
They also funded a huge
research programme at the uni,

538
00:35:43,910 --> 00:35:46,870
and that was the pressure that
got rid of Professor Moriarty

539
00:35:46,950 --> 00:35:49,790
after she started making noises
about Cardinal, too.

540
00:35:49,870 --> 00:35:51,860
But what does that prove, Charlie?

541
00:35:51,950 --> 00:35:54,750
Well, Mentor are entirely funded
by science-based companies.

542
00:35:54,830 --> 00:35:56,350
Do they include Cardinal?

543
00:35:56,430 --> 00:35:58,550
My friend couldn't find that link, but...

544
00:35:58,630 --> 00:36:03,710
- Then how does it relate to our case?
- Well, it's pretty suspicious, Jo.

545
00:36:05,830 --> 00:36:08,220
How did you get started on all this, Charlie?

546
00:36:08,310 --> 00:36:11,030
- Dad suggested I look there.
- Discussing this case with your dad

547
00:36:11,110 --> 00:36:13,180
could cause a retrial in front of another judge.

548
00:36:13,270 --> 00:36:15,420
- Well, he only said...
- I don't care what he said.

549
00:36:15,510 --> 00:36:17,340
He should know better.

550
00:36:22,270 --> 00:36:23,870
What's she so worked up about?

551
00:36:23,950 --> 00:36:27,390
- Difference in approach.
- Are we going to have dinner?

552
00:36:27,830 --> 00:36:30,670
Best wait till after the trial, Mum,
in case I let something slip.

553
00:36:30,750 --> 00:36:32,980
Is there something to let slip?

554
00:36:33,350 --> 00:36:36,500
- How's Neil?
- Still furious with your father.

555
00:36:36,590 --> 00:36:40,870
- Is he going to survive?
- I'm less sure about your father.

556
00:36:53,590 --> 00:36:57,380
Charlie, the intention was good,
but we do have to be so careful.

557
00:36:57,470 --> 00:36:59,540
- I did tell Dad.
- I know.

558
00:37:03,750 --> 00:37:05,710
Why don't you spend the day at Companies House?

559
00:37:05,790 --> 00:37:08,940
- Find out all you can about Mentor.
- What about the work here?

560
00:37:09,030 --> 00:37:12,230
It's the council official talking about the plant.

561
00:37:14,070 --> 00:37:16,870
Charlie, it was a good move.

562
00:37:22,110 --> 00:37:23,910
All rise.

563
00:37:30,870 --> 00:37:32,230
Mrs Mills?

564
00:37:32,310 --> 00:37:35,190
With your leave, my lord, I'd
like to call Richard Stokes.

565
00:37:35,270 --> 00:37:37,630
Mr Stokes is a compliance officer with the council

566
00:37:37,710 --> 00:37:40,430
and investigates residents' complaints.

567
00:37:43,230 --> 00:37:46,350
The Attorney General would
like to see you at 4:15.

568
00:37:46,430 --> 00:37:49,900
- Doesn't he know I'm sitting?
- Yeah. I did point it out.

569
00:37:49,990 --> 00:37:51,310
All right.

570
00:37:56,670 --> 00:37:59,350
I'm sorry, Sir John, the
Attorney is still delayed.

571
00:37:59,430 --> 00:38:03,510
- Are you sure I can't offer you tea?
- I don't think so.

572
00:38:14,350 --> 00:38:18,590
- John, I'm terribly sorry.
- I hope it's worth waiting for.

573
00:38:19,710 --> 00:38:20,910
Please.

574
00:38:26,390 --> 00:38:28,030
Won't you take a seat, John?

575
00:38:28,110 --> 00:38:31,390
If it's not good news, Alan, I
don't need to sit down for it.

576
00:38:31,510 --> 00:38:35,630
The Chief Constable of Sussex
interviewed Rufus Barron personally.

577
00:38:35,710 --> 00:38:38,180
He denied the allegations
against Sir Tim Listfield.

578
00:38:38,270 --> 00:38:40,990
He said he'd made it up so
he could win his court case.

579
00:38:41,070 --> 00:38:43,910
And was it pointed out to him that
he could be charged with perjury?

580
00:38:43,990 --> 00:38:46,460
He was prepared to take that risk.

581
00:38:46,550 --> 00:38:49,990
Well, he's obviously scared.
Somebody's leaned on him.

582
00:38:52,270 --> 00:38:56,390
The Cabinet has referred the matter
of your removal to both Houses.

583
00:38:59,470 --> 00:39:02,940
- Was Haughton present?
- He is a senior member of the Cabinet.

584
00:39:03,150 --> 00:39:06,430
I'm sorry, John, but this
really is the end of the line.

585
00:39:06,510 --> 00:39:09,030
I always thought the hearing came
before the sentence.

586
00:39:09,110 --> 00:39:12,310
The Lord Chancellor requires that you
relinquish the case you're hearing.

587
00:39:12,390 --> 00:39:13,990
I won't give up.

588
00:39:25,910 --> 00:39:28,550
- Hello?
- John, it's Joe, Joe Channing.

589
00:39:28,630 --> 00:39:30,350
Oh, this is a surprise.
- Yeah. Come on up.

590
00:39:51,830 --> 00:39:55,350
John, I was at the club. I heard the news.

591
00:39:58,430 --> 00:40:01,510
- Is it true?
- Oh, yes.

592
00:40:06,390 --> 00:40:08,670
Is there anything to be done?

593
00:40:08,750 --> 00:40:13,430
Well, if you could find some
damning evidence against Haughton.

594
00:40:14,230 --> 00:40:16,590
I can't believe there isn't any.

595
00:40:17,430 --> 00:40:20,350
They're trying to shut me
out of this current trial.

596
00:40:20,430 --> 00:40:23,950
Yes, well, if you were to resist them,

597
00:40:24,030 --> 00:40:26,190
I'm sure they wouldn't want
to undermine the authority

598
00:40:26,195 --> 00:40:27,990
of a High Court Judge in public.
- Ah.

599
00:40:28,070 --> 00:40:31,190
It all stems from that claimant you slept with.

600
00:40:31,870 --> 00:40:34,990
Why didn't you confine yourself
to the female bar?

601
00:40:35,070 --> 00:40:36,430
Oh, Joe.

602
00:40:37,790 --> 00:40:40,940
- What is it about women?
- What?

603
00:40:41,030 --> 00:40:44,550
Well, it's the way they look at you,

604
00:40:45,350 --> 00:40:49,590
the way they touch you, the
softness of their skin,

605
00:40:50,590 --> 00:40:52,580
the smell of their hair.

606
00:40:53,910 --> 00:40:57,830
You long for the flame to finally
extinguish, and then when it does,

607
00:40:57,910 --> 00:41:02,030
you spend your night hours
longing for it to rekindle.

608
00:41:04,230 --> 00:41:05,790
Yes.

609
00:41:06,550 --> 00:41:09,190
Get something on the Home Secretary, John,

610
00:41:09,270 --> 00:41:13,060
and I'm sure your fellow judges
will oppose your impeachment.

611
00:41:20,230 --> 00:41:24,190
John, the court manager's just informed me
that the case isn't going to proceed.

612
00:41:24,270 --> 00:41:26,950
I'd say he was exceeding his authority.

613
00:41:31,950 --> 00:41:34,790
The Lord Chancellor is going to impeach me.

614
00:41:36,430 --> 00:41:38,070
Well, you didn't take them seriously.

615
00:41:38,150 --> 00:41:41,910
Well, I had to find the truth
behind the Haughton allegation.

616
00:41:45,710 --> 00:41:49,630
Charlie's discovered a PR company,
Mentor, is involved in this case.

617
00:41:52,710 --> 00:41:54,150
Who are they?

618
00:41:54,230 --> 00:41:57,310
They've been running a damage
limitation campaign for Cardinal.

619
00:41:57,390 --> 00:42:00,030
It connects both Listfield and Haughton.

620
00:42:01,390 --> 00:42:05,100
- How?
- Both were on the board of Mentor.

621
00:42:05,190 --> 00:42:08,150
Haughton resigned when he joined the Government.

622
00:42:10,670 --> 00:42:14,590
Coop, tell Mrs Channing I'll
see her in court, 10 minutes.

623
00:42:16,630 --> 00:42:20,710
Fine, Mrs Cooper, but this case is a dead duck.

624
00:42:20,790 --> 00:42:23,390
You risk him giving judgment
in favour of the claimants

625
00:42:23,470 --> 00:42:25,620
if you don't appear.

626
00:42:30,230 --> 00:42:33,910
This is your fight, not mine.
I can't see Rufus Barron.

627
00:42:33,990 --> 00:42:35,190
Could help you win this trial.

628
00:42:35,195 --> 00:42:38,825
You just want to swat Neil, Dad.
He's going to marry Mum.

629
00:42:40,510 --> 00:42:43,430
Yep, you're right.
I shouldn't have involved you.

630
00:42:43,510 --> 00:42:45,500
- I'm sorry. I just mean...
- No, no, no. Come on.

631
00:42:45,590 --> 00:42:47,500
We've got a trial to conduct.

632
00:42:47,590 --> 00:42:48,910
John!

633
00:42:49,790 --> 00:42:54,030
John, I've just had Rochester on
saying I must stop you

634
00:42:54,110 --> 00:42:56,500
until the debate for your removal is heard.

635
00:42:56,590 --> 00:42:59,030
And what was your response, Monty?

636
00:42:59,110 --> 00:43:02,070
You're a High Court Judge until that happens.

637
00:43:10,790 --> 00:43:13,430
If this is about the case, I'll hear you.
If not, I won't.

638
00:43:13,510 --> 00:43:17,140
It's about the case which you should
no longer be hearing.

639
00:43:17,790 --> 00:43:20,430
If you want to withdraw your defence
in favour of the claimant,

640
00:43:20,510 --> 00:43:22,550
please feel free to do so.

641
00:43:26,990 --> 00:43:29,270
No? Right. Mrs Mills?

642
00:43:29,350 --> 00:43:33,310
With your leave, my lord, we'd like to call
Professor Vang Pao.

643
00:43:33,390 --> 00:43:37,230
Professor Pao is a lecturer
and a practising paediatrician

644
00:43:37,310 --> 00:43:39,590
in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam.

645
00:43:40,950 --> 00:43:44,870
Professor Pao, are you one of,
if not the leading authority

646
00:43:44,950 --> 00:43:47,670
on babies affected by dioxins?
- Yes.

647
00:43:48,870 --> 00:43:52,260
In the '60s, the US military persistently sprayed

648
00:43:52,350 --> 00:43:55,900
my country with a defoliant known as Agent Orange.

649
00:43:56,790 --> 00:44:00,230
Its legacy is, sadly, many babies born dead

650
00:44:00,310 --> 00:44:03,150
or with mutagenic or teratogenic deformities.

651
00:44:03,790 --> 00:44:05,910
Can you tell the court what these consist of?

652
00:44:05,990 --> 00:44:10,750
Children with malformed spines, undeveloped limbs,
fused organs,

653
00:44:11,270 --> 00:44:15,630
often no eyes, noses, mouths or genitalia.

654
00:44:16,110 --> 00:44:19,030
Can you tell us how these deformities are caused?

655
00:44:19,110 --> 00:44:22,310
Dioxins are the most toxic chemicals known to man.

656
00:44:23,030 --> 00:44:25,340
They are bio accumulative.

657
00:44:25,430 --> 00:44:30,510
Once entering the water and food chain,
they increase their concentration

658
00:44:30,590 --> 00:44:32,710
and become impossible to eradicate.

659
00:44:32,790 --> 00:44:36,710
When breathed, ingested
or absorbed through the skin

660
00:44:36,790 --> 00:44:40,630
they cause cells to mutate,
especially those of a foetus.

661
00:44:40,710 --> 00:44:45,030
These divide and destroy the immune
and central nervous systems.

662
00:44:45,350 --> 00:44:48,630
Do you come up against its effects
in your medical practice?

663
00:44:48,710 --> 00:44:49,910
Yes, I do.

664
00:44:49,990 --> 00:44:54,190
Even though the war in Vietnam was
brought to an end 35 years ago.

665
00:44:55,350 --> 00:44:58,310
My patients are second-generation children

666
00:44:58,390 --> 00:45:00,430
suffering from dioxin poisoning.

667
00:45:00,510 --> 00:45:03,270
Is there anything to be done about this toxicity?

668
00:45:03,350 --> 00:45:08,220
Most urgently we must stop producing and
spreading dioxins further in the world.

669
00:45:09,270 --> 00:45:13,950
Is there any doubt that dioxins cause
teratogenic deformities in babies

670
00:45:14,030 --> 00:45:16,260
or mothers to miscarry?

671
00:45:16,350 --> 00:45:18,180
Repeated case studies show

672
00:45:18,270 --> 00:45:22,590
that many women exposed to dioxins
in the form of aerial spraying

673
00:45:22,670 --> 00:45:26,110
either miscarried or produced mutagenic babies.

674
00:45:26,190 --> 00:45:30,110
Were these mothers in direct contact
with the spraying?

675
00:45:30,670 --> 00:45:31,820
No, sir.

676
00:45:31,910 --> 00:45:36,190
Some are wives of workers where
dioxins in herbicides are used.

677
00:45:36,270 --> 00:45:38,630
Also large numbers of families of servicemen

678
00:45:38,710 --> 00:45:41,470
who went to Vietnam are affected.

679
00:45:41,550 --> 00:45:42,870
Thank you.

680
00:45:43,350 --> 00:45:46,660
Professor Pao, did you examine baby Henri Peacock?

681
00:45:46,750 --> 00:45:49,550
- I did.
- Would you tell us what you found?

682
00:45:50,150 --> 00:45:54,510
The little girl is suffering irreparable damage
of the spine.

683
00:45:55,070 --> 00:45:58,910
Each of the vertebrae has a 60% distortion.

684
00:45:59,590 --> 00:46:02,230
The nervous system is malfunctioning.

685
00:46:02,590 --> 00:46:04,820
She has one eye only.

686
00:46:04,910 --> 00:46:08,220
Her external organs of smell
and hearing are absent

687
00:46:08,630 --> 00:46:10,910
and she has a cleft palate.

688
00:46:11,270 --> 00:46:14,150
In your opinion, what caused these problems?

689
00:46:14,630 --> 00:46:19,420
One can never be absolute,
but with some degree of certainty

690
00:46:19,510 --> 00:46:23,590
the cause was the parents
breathing or ingesting dioxins

691
00:46:23,670 --> 00:46:26,190
over months or years before birth.

692
00:46:26,270 --> 00:46:29,630
These would accumulate
and affect the endocrine system,

693
00:46:29,710 --> 00:46:32,390
their ability to reproduce effectively.

694
00:46:32,470 --> 00:46:35,780
Are these likely to go on
affecting them, Professor?

695
00:46:36,270 --> 00:46:38,030
For all their lives.

696
00:46:39,750 --> 00:46:42,430
They will never reproduce successfully.

697
00:46:48,350 --> 00:46:51,230
You stated the incineration plant was the source

698
00:46:51,310 --> 00:46:53,780
of the Peacock's contamination.

699
00:46:53,870 --> 00:47:00,110
If the parents had been in contact with pesticides
containing dioxins,

700
00:47:00,190 --> 00:47:03,190
would you be as confident?
- Other sources of contamination

701
00:47:03,270 --> 00:47:06,350
would make the plant only a contributing factor.

702
00:47:06,430 --> 00:47:11,190
If one parent were in contact with dioxins,

703
00:47:11,270 --> 00:47:14,630
say through working with treated timber,

704
00:47:15,350 --> 00:47:18,470
could the same consequences follow?
- Of course.

705
00:47:18,550 --> 00:47:21,990
As with American babies whose
fathers were in Vietnam.

706
00:47:22,070 --> 00:47:24,540
Were you paid to come here and give evidence?

707
00:47:24,630 --> 00:47:28,070
- Yes. I received payment.
- You had your fare paid for?

708
00:47:28,430 --> 00:47:29,500
Yes.

709
00:47:29,910 --> 00:47:34,150
I lecture in paediatric
toxicology at Leeds University.

710
00:47:34,230 --> 00:47:37,070
Giving evidence coincided with my lectures.

711
00:47:37,430 --> 00:47:41,140
- Giving paid evidence.
- How much were you paid, Professor?

712
00:47:41,230 --> 00:47:44,190
The same for conducting my clinic in Vietnam.

713
00:47:44,270 --> 00:47:47,110
The exchange is about $5 a day.

714
00:47:48,510 --> 00:47:50,900
Any more questions, Mrs Channing?

715
00:47:51,550 --> 00:47:54,070
With your taking over the questioning, hardly.

716
00:47:54,150 --> 00:47:57,190
And make the most of it while you can, my lord.

717
00:47:59,670 --> 00:48:00,990
Mrs Mills?

718
00:48:10,790 --> 00:48:12,150
Thank you.

719
00:48:28,350 --> 00:48:29,670
Hello?

720
00:48:30,430 --> 00:48:33,820
Rufus Barron? It's Charlie Deed.

721
00:48:33,910 --> 00:48:36,630
I already told you on the phone,
I don't want to talk to you.

722
00:48:36,710 --> 00:48:38,990
Please. Just one minute. One minute, that's all.

723
00:48:39,070 --> 00:48:41,060
You're wasting your time.

724
00:48:53,910 --> 00:48:55,230
Thank you.

725
00:48:58,950 --> 00:49:02,030
- Hi, I'm Charlie Deed.
- Go away or I'll call the police.

726
00:49:02,110 --> 00:49:04,790
- If you do that I could get disbarred.
- Then you'd best get going.

727
00:49:04,870 --> 00:49:07,310
- Five minutes. That's all.
- Five?

728
00:49:08,150 --> 00:49:11,670
- You only wanted one.
- Look, you don't have to let me in.

729
00:49:11,750 --> 00:49:15,110
We can talk through the door.
Please. It's important.

730
00:49:15,830 --> 00:49:17,270
Five minutes.

731
00:49:19,630 --> 00:49:22,670
But I don't know anything about Cardinal Waste.

732
00:49:24,390 --> 00:49:26,430
Hey, this is a great flat.

733
00:49:28,230 --> 00:49:32,620
- You collect paintings?
- Well, none of them are valuable.

734
00:49:34,230 --> 00:49:36,790
I suppose this one might be one day.

735
00:49:38,310 --> 00:49:40,620
I've got two of her paintings.

736
00:49:44,230 --> 00:49:46,460
Where did you learn about art?

737
00:49:47,910 --> 00:49:50,470
- From Tim Listfield?
- You're kidding.

738
00:49:50,790 --> 00:49:53,910
I taught him everything.
Still he knows nothing.

739
00:49:54,670 --> 00:49:56,150
It's true.

740
00:49:56,230 --> 00:49:59,700
Oh, he buys paintings and
knows how to get good deals.

741
00:49:59,790 --> 00:50:01,150
That's all.

742
00:50:01,590 --> 00:50:05,270
He never went to an art gallery
or a theatre, come to that,

743
00:50:05,510 --> 00:50:08,390
unless it was to meet someone to bribe them.

744
00:50:08,470 --> 00:50:11,150
Why did you withdraw your allegation
about the bribe?

745
00:50:11,230 --> 00:50:13,790
You sure you're not a journalist?

746
00:50:13,870 --> 00:50:15,630
They've been dogging me ever since the case.

747
00:50:15,635 --> 00:50:19,475
I told you, I'm a junior barrister
in the case against Cardinal.

748
00:50:23,990 --> 00:50:25,310
Look.

749
00:50:26,790 --> 00:50:29,550
- That's not a living baby?
- Mmm-hmm.

750
00:50:31,190 --> 00:50:32,950
She's 18 months old.

751
00:50:33,630 --> 00:50:37,470
It's caused by dioxins belching out
of Cardinal's incinerator.

752
00:50:38,030 --> 00:50:39,790
That's really gross.

753
00:50:41,430 --> 00:50:44,580
Mentor PR are telling everyone it doesn't happen,

754
00:50:45,190 --> 00:50:47,710
that Cardinal are innocent.

755
00:50:47,790 --> 00:50:50,830
Mentor would have made Adolf Hitler sound good.

756
00:50:52,230 --> 00:50:53,710
You know them?

757
00:51:03,110 --> 00:51:04,390
- Yep.
- Dad?

758
00:51:04,470 --> 00:51:06,460
- Yeah?
- Is it okay to come up?

759
00:51:06,550 --> 00:51:07,870
Sure.

760
00:51:20,830 --> 00:51:23,110
- Are you sure it's okay?
- Yeah.

761
00:51:29,030 --> 00:51:30,430
Hiya.

762
00:51:30,910 --> 00:51:32,590
Are you getting paranoid or do you imagine

763
00:51:32,595 --> 00:51:34,830
I'm having the sort of social life I'm not having?

764
00:51:34,910 --> 00:51:36,470
I've just talked to Rufus Barron.

765
00:51:36,550 --> 00:51:39,940
He does have evidence about
Listfield bribing Neil.

766
00:51:40,030 --> 00:51:42,310
That's not what he told the Chief Constable.

767
00:51:42,390 --> 00:51:45,940
- He's dead scared, Dad.
- And with good reason.

768
00:51:47,350 --> 00:51:49,500
How did you get him to talk?

769
00:51:50,390 --> 00:51:53,750
I persuaded him you're the
only one who can help him.

770
00:51:53,830 --> 00:51:55,980
He's really upset about baby Henri.

771
00:51:56,070 --> 00:51:57,950
He's going to try and find something on Mentor.

772
00:51:57,955 --> 00:52:01,425
Don't talk to me about the case.
Do you want some soup?

773
00:52:05,030 --> 00:52:08,870
Ask Mr Barron to come to the chambers
at the adjournment, would you, Coop?

774
00:52:08,950 --> 00:52:11,070
He was a defendant, Judge.

775
00:52:11,150 --> 00:52:13,750
It's a bit late to worry about niceties, Coop.

776
00:52:17,310 --> 00:52:20,030
Mr Peacock, since your involvement in the campaign

777
00:52:20,110 --> 00:52:22,150
against Cardinal's incinerator,

778
00:52:22,230 --> 00:52:25,190
how many trips have you made
to the plant to count lorries?

779
00:52:25,270 --> 00:52:28,310
I don't know.
I didn't think to count at first.

780
00:52:28,670 --> 00:52:31,550
But about 180 since I started
keeping detailed records.

781
00:52:31,630 --> 00:52:33,030
If you look at your notes,

782
00:52:33,110 --> 00:52:36,900
can you tell the court how they differ
from the company's records?

783
00:52:36,990 --> 00:52:40,220
Well, the company records,
they show only two lorry loads

784
00:52:40,310 --> 00:52:43,910
of hospital waste coming in a month.
- What item is that?

785
00:52:45,310 --> 00:52:47,460
Eleven in folder B, my lord.

786
00:52:49,070 --> 00:52:51,830
They had 908 loads of rubbish in July.

787
00:52:52,390 --> 00:52:55,670
Only two lorries with hospital waste, supposedly.

788
00:52:55,750 --> 00:52:58,550
But according to the records we got
from inside the plant,

789
00:52:58,630 --> 00:53:00,860
they had nearer to 28.

790
00:53:00,950 --> 00:53:03,910
That's 26 more than the permitted quota.

791
00:53:03,990 --> 00:53:07,300
How did you get these records
from inside the plant?

792
00:53:10,590 --> 00:53:13,550
We found someone on the inside who helped us.

793
00:53:15,230 --> 00:53:17,910
Are you calling this person, Mrs Mills?

794
00:53:18,750 --> 00:53:22,300
No, my lord.
The person won't be identified.

795
00:53:23,790 --> 00:53:24,940
I see.

796
00:53:25,790 --> 00:53:27,670
Thank you, Mr Peacock.

797
00:53:29,190 --> 00:53:31,830
- You're a thief and a liar.
- What?

798
00:53:32,590 --> 00:53:36,190
- Do you have a criminal record?
- Well, no, not really.

799
00:53:36,270 --> 00:53:38,500
Oh, not really, but maybe.

800
00:53:40,150 --> 00:53:42,750
Well, yes. I was only 19.

801
00:53:43,150 --> 00:53:47,700
Ah, Mrs Channing, in civil proceedings,
cross-examination of spent convictions

802
00:53:47,790 --> 00:53:52,710
is prohibited under Section 4 of the
Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974.

803
00:53:52,790 --> 00:53:54,150
It may well be spent,

804
00:53:54,230 --> 00:53:57,510
but you have the discretion to permit me
to put his record to him.

805
00:53:57,590 --> 00:54:00,670
I'm asking you to exercise that
in favour of my client

806
00:54:00,750 --> 00:54:03,110
because of the importance
of this witness's credibility.

807
00:54:03,190 --> 00:54:05,660
My lord, Mr Peacock has not since offended

808
00:54:05,750 --> 00:54:08,310
and there's no reason to assume
that he's not telling the truth.

809
00:54:08,390 --> 00:54:13,340
Well, the credibility of the witness
is plainly relevant

810
00:54:13,430 --> 00:54:14,830
to Mrs Channing's case. No.

811
00:54:14,910 --> 00:54:17,790
I give leave to adduce the witness's record.

812
00:54:19,030 --> 00:54:22,950
Mr Peacock, can you tell the court
how you came by information

813
00:54:23,030 --> 00:54:26,950
from inside Cardinal's plant?
- It was passed on to me by a worker.

814
00:54:27,030 --> 00:54:29,500
- We only have your word for that.
- It's true.

815
00:54:29,590 --> 00:54:32,900
Didn't you simply concoct these figures
to suit your argument?

816
00:54:32,990 --> 00:54:36,350
- No. I didn't.
- You lied before. Why not now?

817
00:54:36,430 --> 00:54:39,430
My lie wasn't hurting no one, not like theirs.

818
00:54:39,990 --> 00:54:42,670
There's no evidence
that Cardinal have lied about anything.

819
00:54:42,750 --> 00:54:44,790
They're poisoning people!

820
00:54:46,030 --> 00:54:49,030
When you were picketing the incinerator plant,

821
00:54:49,270 --> 00:54:52,230
did you lose your temper with the workers?

822
00:54:52,310 --> 00:54:56,150
- I don't remember. I was pretty angry.
- You get angry very quickly.

823
00:54:56,230 --> 00:54:58,140
So would you be if that happened to your child.

824
00:54:58,230 --> 00:54:59,950
On one of your visits to the plant,

825
00:55:00,030 --> 00:55:03,870
did you fly into a rage and
threaten to kill the manager?

826
00:55:03,950 --> 00:55:05,470
He's killing us.

827
00:55:05,550 --> 00:55:08,070
Did you threaten to get even,

828
00:55:08,150 --> 00:55:10,380
to get the plant closed down?
- What if I did?

829
00:55:10,470 --> 00:55:14,180
If the plant was so damaging,
why didn't you move away?

830
00:55:14,270 --> 00:55:17,030
No! This is our home.

831
00:55:17,110 --> 00:55:19,710
I'll tear that plant down
brick by brick before that.

832
00:55:19,790 --> 00:55:21,830
Then you care little for
the health of your family?

833
00:55:21,835 --> 00:55:24,550
- No. They're everything.
- But you wouldn't move away.

834
00:55:24,630 --> 00:55:27,510
You can't say that.
- Who do you think you are?

835
00:55:27,590 --> 00:55:29,580
You people, you don't even care who you hurt.

836
00:55:29,670 --> 00:55:33,910
Mr Peacock, I think you should
step outside and calm yourself.

837
00:55:42,110 --> 00:55:44,020
They're beating us, aren't they?

838
00:55:44,110 --> 00:55:45,940
They attacked your credibility, that's all.

839
00:55:46,030 --> 00:55:47,940
How do they get that stuff?

840
00:55:48,030 --> 00:55:51,310
They spend a lot of money
on investigators, corrupt officials.

841
00:55:51,390 --> 00:55:55,990
- I'd like to wring their necks.
- Do you think that we can win, Jo?

842
00:55:56,070 --> 00:56:00,110
I did say it wouldn't be easy,
not with what's at stake, but...

843
00:56:00,190 --> 00:56:02,750
What about Henri? Could you bring her into court?

844
00:56:02,830 --> 00:56:06,950
- She needs constant attention.
- It might be helpful if you can.

845
00:56:07,710 --> 00:56:12,260
You! You think you're so superior,
don't you? You're just filth.

846
00:56:12,350 --> 00:56:14,260
- I'm sorry?
- We're going to ruin you.

847
00:56:14,350 --> 00:56:16,070
- Oh, drop it.
- Paul, please leave it.

848
00:56:16,150 --> 00:56:18,060
And, you, our council tax pays you.

849
00:56:18,150 --> 00:56:20,910
- Why don't you calm down?
- It's not your home under attack, is it?

850
00:56:20,990 --> 00:56:22,910
- The council did offer to re-house you.
- Guard.

851
00:56:22,990 --> 00:56:24,710
Yeah. What about Henri, eh?

852
00:56:24,790 --> 00:56:26,470
How are you going to make her whole again?

853
00:56:26,475 --> 00:56:27,550
- Oh, grow up.
- You...

854
00:56:27,630 --> 00:56:30,990
- Paul, Paul, they're not worth it.
- Calm down.

855
00:56:43,390 --> 00:56:46,540
I didn't think I'd ever come-back here willingly.

856
00:56:48,350 --> 00:56:49,830
I must be mad.

857
00:56:52,990 --> 00:56:56,030
- Mr Barron, Judge.
- Thanks, Coop.

858
00:56:57,470 --> 00:57:01,260
Up until September last year,
I was personal assistant

859
00:57:01,350 --> 00:57:03,990
to the businessman Sir Tim Listfield.

860
00:57:04,830 --> 00:57:07,350
He prosecuted me for theft,

861
00:57:07,430 --> 00:57:11,950
but in fact he gave me money
to put in a secret account, 3.7 million.

862
00:57:13,430 --> 00:57:15,500
Tim's a billionaire.

863
00:57:15,590 --> 00:57:19,630
An extra £700,000 was added
to the charge already against me.

864
00:57:19,710 --> 00:57:22,270
This was the money I had to withdraw in cash

865
00:57:22,350 --> 00:57:27,220
which Tim gave to the then Secretary
of State for Trade, Neil Haughton.

866
00:57:28,190 --> 00:57:30,100
Why did he give him the money?

867
00:57:30,190 --> 00:57:34,190
As a bribe, to get him
to sign off on all the false billing

868
00:57:34,270 --> 00:57:36,630
his company was making on a defence contract,

869
00:57:36,710 --> 00:57:38,780
a new radar system for the navy.

870
00:57:39,190 --> 00:57:42,030
He was in with the judge for about an hour.

871
00:57:43,750 --> 00:57:47,540
- This is Barron's whole statement.
- Thanks, Coop.

872
00:57:47,630 --> 00:57:49,230
What do you want done with it?

873
00:57:49,310 --> 00:57:53,070
Take it to the Attorney General.
That way I know it's there.

874
00:57:56,150 --> 00:57:59,350
I'll see the Attorney gets
the transcript, Mrs Cooper.

875
00:57:59,430 --> 00:58:03,110
- The judge asked me to get a receipt.
- That's most unusual.

876
00:58:04,670 --> 00:58:05,990
Very well.

877
00:58:25,830 --> 00:58:28,220
It's the key to the self-storage
lock-up on Brighton Road.

878
00:58:28,310 --> 00:58:29,750
Will it still be open?

879
00:58:29,830 --> 00:58:31,900
- Till 10:00.
- Great.

880
00:59:03,870 --> 00:59:05,270
Is this all evidence?

881
00:59:05,350 --> 00:59:07,470
Every piece of paper from
the navy's radar contract.

882
00:59:07,550 --> 00:59:09,460
Could you take notes, please?

883
00:59:09,550 --> 00:59:12,550
There is a number of clear plastic boxes.

884
00:59:12,630 --> 00:59:14,070
And this one,

885
00:59:16,310 --> 00:59:19,830
appears to contain photocopies

886
00:59:20,550 --> 00:59:24,550
of invoices relating to MOD contracts.

887
00:59:24,950 --> 00:59:26,310
This one's got my diaries.

888
00:59:26,390 --> 00:59:28,590
Well, there's far too many for us
to take with us now.

889
00:59:28,670 --> 00:59:31,070
We'll come back for them. I'll
take the diaries with me now.

890
00:59:31,075 --> 00:59:33,070
Will you make a note of the number of boxes,

891
00:59:33,110 --> 00:59:35,030
the date, the time, the address of the lock-up,

892
00:59:35,035 --> 00:59:38,715
and the fact that Mr Barron is willing
for us to take them?

893
00:59:40,470 --> 00:59:41,590
Paul?

894
00:59:49,590 --> 00:59:52,470
- What's the matter?
- Is Henri okay?

895
00:59:52,910 --> 00:59:54,430
She's sleeping.

896
00:59:56,350 --> 00:59:57,470
Paul?

897
01:00:01,790 --> 01:00:03,830
What if we're wrong, Paul?

898
01:00:05,110 --> 01:00:06,790
What if it was me?

899
01:00:07,910 --> 01:00:10,550
You know, working with wood treatment?

900
01:00:15,950 --> 01:00:18,590
I think you should turn this evidence
straight over to the police.

901
01:00:18,595 --> 01:00:20,260
Well, it's interesting,

902
01:00:20,350 --> 01:00:23,710
but I'm not sure that these diaries
even amount to evidence.

903
01:00:23,790 --> 01:00:26,260
We don't know yet whether the files
are going to provide

904
01:00:26,350 --> 01:00:29,950
any evidence against Haughton.
- So then you're no further forward.

905
01:00:30,030 --> 01:00:32,260
They don't know that, do they?

906
01:00:34,070 --> 01:00:37,110
Does Barron's transcript incriminate
the Home Secretary?

907
01:00:37,190 --> 01:00:38,710
No, it doesn't.

908
01:00:39,550 --> 01:00:43,830
Deed isn't asking for a trial.
- He wants a judicial enquiry,

909
01:00:44,630 --> 01:00:46,510
which we might have to give him.

910
01:00:46,590 --> 01:00:48,990
Mr Barron was plausible enough
for a jury to have freed him,

911
01:00:49,030 --> 01:00:52,500
and now it seems that Deed has evidence
to support his allegations.

912
01:00:52,590 --> 01:00:55,510
The Home Secretary assures me
he has no evidence.

913
01:00:55,590 --> 01:00:57,740
We should push on with his removal.

914
01:00:57,830 --> 01:01:01,950
If you could make it go any
faster, Ian, I'm sure you would.

915
01:01:02,030 --> 01:01:05,950
I'm concerned Parliament debates this
before prorogation.

916
01:01:06,030 --> 01:01:07,030
Thank you for this.

917
01:01:07,350 --> 01:01:11,900
Mr Peacock, while accepting the
tragedy of your daughter's condition,

918
01:01:11,990 --> 01:01:17,670
isn't it true to say that this is of
your own making rather than Cardinal's?

919
01:01:17,950 --> 01:01:21,580
No. They were burning waste
they shouldn't have been burning.

920
01:01:21,670 --> 01:01:25,380
You know this from your mysterious insider?

921
01:01:25,470 --> 01:01:29,310
- Yes.
- Whose documents you don't have?

922
01:01:29,390 --> 01:01:30,990
They were stolen.

923
01:01:31,070 --> 01:01:36,390
If, as you claim, you have evidence
that the incinerator is the cause

924
01:01:36,470 --> 01:01:40,750
of you and your wife's health
problems, why didn't you move away?

925
01:01:40,830 --> 01:01:44,270
How could we? We can't afford to move.

926
01:01:45,070 --> 01:01:47,590
The cottage is worthless
thanks to the incinerator.

927
01:01:47,670 --> 01:01:51,060
Isn't this whole campaign to cover the fact

928
01:01:51,150 --> 01:01:54,300
that this misfortune was of your own making?

929
01:01:55,110 --> 01:01:57,180
You worked with chemicals.

930
01:01:57,270 --> 01:02:00,630
- You brought them home.
- No. No, it's not.

931
01:02:00,710 --> 01:02:03,990
You wanted so much
for the incinerator to be the cause.

932
01:02:04,070 --> 01:02:05,670
It is the cause.

933
01:02:07,590 --> 01:02:11,430
They blighted little Henri's life, our lives.

934
01:02:12,510 --> 01:02:16,140
Looking after her 24 hours a day,
never any respite.

935
01:02:16,950 --> 01:02:18,990
Can't you see it's the incinerator?

936
01:02:19,070 --> 01:02:21,710
- It is!
- Thank you, Mr Peacock.

937
01:02:24,030 --> 01:02:26,390
Thank you, Mr Peacock.
- You can step down.

938
01:02:26,470 --> 01:02:28,940
The council won't give us any help.

939
01:02:29,390 --> 01:02:34,020
They just want to stick Henri in a home.
- We won't let that happen.

940
01:02:34,110 --> 01:02:35,710
Thank you.

941
01:02:40,910 --> 01:02:44,830
The courier service say all the boxes
in the lock-up have gone.

942
01:02:45,910 --> 01:02:47,430
Where? How?

943
01:02:50,070 --> 01:02:51,510
Thank you.

944
01:02:53,230 --> 01:02:55,510
An urgent matter not related
to this case has come up.

945
01:02:55,590 --> 01:02:59,190
- I'm going to take a short adjournment.
- All rise.

946
01:03:00,350 --> 01:03:02,260
Any further explanation, Coop?

947
01:03:02,350 --> 01:03:06,060
Perhaps Sir Ian Rochester has one.
He's waiting, Judge.

948
01:03:07,190 --> 01:03:09,100
Did you call Mr Barron?

949
01:03:16,270 --> 01:03:19,820
John, do you actually have any
evidence against Haughton?

950
01:03:19,910 --> 01:03:23,300
- Do you know that I don't?
- I know no such thing.

951
01:03:24,510 --> 01:03:26,070
I have Barron's diaries.

952
01:03:27,510 --> 01:03:30,230
Are these another part of his fanciful world?

953
01:03:32,870 --> 01:03:35,950
The Leader of the House has set
a timetable for your removal,

954
01:03:36,030 --> 01:03:40,310
before prorogation of Parliament.
- That seems indecently hasty.

955
01:03:40,830 --> 01:03:42,430
Yes, doesn't it?

956
01:03:42,870 --> 01:03:46,630
Unless and until it succeeds,
I'm still a High Court Judge.

957
01:03:46,710 --> 01:03:48,940
I do beg your pardon, my lord.

958
01:03:50,470 --> 01:03:53,670
Will you have heard the Cardinal case before then?

959
01:03:54,870 --> 01:03:57,790
That would depend upon how well
prepared the defence is.

960
01:03:57,870 --> 01:04:01,790
If not, the trial will have to be
abandoned and a retrial set.

961
01:04:01,870 --> 01:04:06,190
Not unless the consent of the Queen
is given for my removal.

962
01:04:07,430 --> 01:04:09,190
- Yes.
- Yes.

963
01:04:10,510 --> 01:04:12,390
Thank you, Mrs Cooper.

964
01:04:16,550 --> 01:04:20,470
I'd be surprised if he didn't know
about that missing evidence.

965
01:04:21,590 --> 01:04:24,630
Get me the Chief Constable,
would you please, Coop?

966
01:04:24,710 --> 01:04:26,860
- And ask Charlie to step in.
- Sure.

967
01:04:27,670 --> 01:04:30,630
Charlie, the judge would like a word.

968
01:04:31,510 --> 01:04:33,740
Is he under a lot of pressure?

969
01:04:35,670 --> 01:04:36,900
Yep?

970
01:04:39,270 --> 01:04:41,710
Don't go, Coop. This is business.

971
01:04:41,870 --> 01:04:44,230
- Do you want some coffee?
- Are you all right?

972
01:04:44,310 --> 01:04:46,460
We need to see Rufus Barron.

973
01:04:47,870 --> 01:04:49,990
There must have been something
caught on CCTV camera.

974
01:04:50,030 --> 01:04:51,630
Not according to the Chief Constable.

975
01:04:51,710 --> 01:04:54,470
The van that took the boxes away
had no registration plates,

976
01:04:54,550 --> 01:04:56,230
had no distinguishing features.

977
01:04:56,310 --> 01:05:00,470
- We should have taken them last night.
- Yeah. You're probably right.

978
01:05:01,110 --> 01:05:02,630
Are you sure there's nothing else?

979
01:05:02,710 --> 01:05:06,310
Why do you think I went to
so much trouble hiding it all?

980
01:05:09,750 --> 01:05:11,070
I'm stuck.

981
01:05:19,150 --> 01:05:24,020
Look, I have the access codes
to the Mentor offices.

982
01:05:24,550 --> 01:05:27,910
You could still defeat Listfield
if I found evidence.

983
01:05:28,390 --> 01:05:32,990
I can't possibly sanction what you're suggesting.

984
01:05:41,030 --> 01:05:42,510
I'll call you.

985
01:05:45,270 --> 01:05:47,630
Cardinal is an ethical company.

986
01:05:47,710 --> 01:05:50,830
Managing the country's waste
is an ethical pursuit.

987
01:05:50,910 --> 01:05:53,590
Our little nation produces
enough rubbish annually

988
01:05:53,670 --> 01:05:57,830
to put a blanket three inches thick
over the entire English Channel.

989
01:05:59,030 --> 01:06:02,500
How did you respond to the campaign
against Peaks Farm?

990
01:06:02,750 --> 01:06:06,540
I was shocked.
- We care about the community we live in.

991
01:06:07,510 --> 01:06:11,470
As managing director of
Cardinal Waste Management PLC,

992
01:06:11,550 --> 01:06:14,670
where do you live, Sir Peter?
- In a village near Guildford.

993
01:06:14,750 --> 01:06:18,510
Sir Peter, you may find it appropriate
to walk around your village

994
01:06:18,590 --> 01:06:20,630
near Guildford with your hands
in your pockets,

995
01:06:20,710 --> 01:06:23,150
but not in this courtroom, please.

996
01:06:24,590 --> 01:06:25,870
My lord.

997
01:06:27,430 --> 01:06:30,310
Do you have an incinerator plant
across from your back garden?

998
01:06:30,390 --> 01:06:31,670
No.

999
01:06:31,750 --> 01:06:35,350
You need infrastructure
for such a plant, a harbour, ideally.

1000
01:06:35,430 --> 01:06:38,070
So when you say you care
about the community you live in,

1001
01:06:38,150 --> 01:06:41,460
you weren't actually talking about you.
- I was speaking about the company.

1002
01:06:41,550 --> 01:06:44,020
Whose plant you prefer not to have
in your backyard?

1003
01:06:44,110 --> 01:06:46,950
If we wish to live as we do, we
must accept the consequences,

1004
01:06:47,030 --> 01:06:48,310
make sacrifices.

1005
01:06:48,390 --> 01:06:53,100
But while some people make the profits
and live in pleasant villages,

1006
01:06:53,190 --> 01:06:57,870
other people have to live with the risks
and make the sacrifices, don't they?

1007
01:06:58,070 --> 01:06:59,830
It's perfectly safe.

1008
01:07:01,230 --> 01:07:04,590
Do you have children, Sir Peter?
Perfectly well and whole children?

1009
01:07:04,670 --> 01:07:08,430
- It is a clean, harmless process.
- That you don't live near.

1010
01:07:09,070 --> 01:07:11,270
My lord, where is this going?

1011
01:07:12,190 --> 01:07:13,950
Move on, Mrs Mills.

1012
01:07:14,910 --> 01:07:17,350
Two summers ago, did you have
a plague of flies at the plant?

1013
01:07:17,355 --> 01:07:18,870
We dealt with those very swiftly.

1014
01:07:18,950 --> 01:07:21,830
By spraying insecticide so that your neighbours

1015
01:07:21,910 --> 01:07:23,950
had to keep their windows shut.
- Mmm-hmm.

1016
01:07:24,030 --> 01:07:27,030
And then there are the smells that
people are constantly complaining about.

1017
01:07:27,035 --> 01:07:31,070
Your clients come from farming stock.
Farms aren't smell free.

1018
01:07:31,150 --> 01:07:35,590
The rats, the flies, the smells,
the thundering traffic on the roads

1019
01:07:35,670 --> 01:07:39,510
as 30-ton lorries carry piles
of rubbish to be burnt,

1020
01:07:39,590 --> 01:07:42,550
these are all tangible pollutants, Sir Peter.

1021
01:07:43,830 --> 01:07:47,950
But the real problem that Mr
and Mr Peacock can't avoid

1022
01:07:48,030 --> 01:07:52,790
are the deadly dioxins
that float invisibly from your chimneys,

1023
01:07:52,870 --> 01:07:54,230
polluting all they touch,

1024
01:07:54,310 --> 01:07:57,310
damaging the map of human
existence for generations,

1025
01:07:57,390 --> 01:07:59,430
and robbing Mr and Mrs Peacock

1026
01:07:59,510 --> 01:08:03,870
of any chance of ever having
a normal, healthy family.

1027
01:08:06,310 --> 01:08:10,430
There are no dangerous, insidious
dioxins escaping from Peaks Farm.

1028
01:08:10,950 --> 01:08:13,020
Can you be sure, Professor Neame?

1029
01:08:13,110 --> 01:08:14,750
We make this our full time study

1030
01:08:14,830 --> 01:08:18,030
at the Institute of Research
and Clinical Practice at Oxford.

1031
01:08:18,110 --> 01:08:20,630
We train world experts in the field.

1032
01:08:21,190 --> 01:08:24,070
Are you saying dioxins aren't harmful?

1033
01:08:24,750 --> 01:08:27,630
Some are deadly, especially at grade one.

1034
01:08:28,070 --> 01:08:31,590
Such dioxins aren't issuing
from Cardinal's chimneys.

1035
01:08:32,230 --> 01:08:35,270
But dioxins were found in and around the site

1036
01:08:35,350 --> 01:08:37,790
and at the Peacock's house nearby.

1037
01:08:38,550 --> 01:08:41,110
We have to look to the previous use for the site,

1038
01:08:41,190 --> 01:08:42,950
a timber importer.

1039
01:08:43,030 --> 01:08:46,340
Most likely the dioxins came from treated wood.

1040
01:08:46,430 --> 01:08:49,070
Scrap wood burnt in the yard at low temperatures

1041
01:08:49,150 --> 01:08:51,750
would have caused dioxins to migrate.

1042
01:08:52,590 --> 01:08:53,870
What a day!

1043
01:08:53,950 --> 01:08:56,270
He's trying to make the most
of his last gasp on the bench

1044
01:08:56,350 --> 01:08:58,820
by sitting a longer day than any other judge.

1045
01:08:58,910 --> 01:09:01,060
He won't get this done before he's removed.

1046
01:09:01,150 --> 01:09:04,510
I wouldn't count on it
being his last breath, George.

1047
01:09:06,630 --> 01:09:07,860
Charlie?

1048
01:09:12,710 --> 01:09:15,430
Dad, it's me. I'm going to go and see Rufus.

1049
01:09:24,790 --> 01:09:26,830
There's a lot of security.

1050
01:09:27,950 --> 01:09:30,260
This is as close as I should go.

1051
01:09:31,110 --> 01:09:34,070
Do you think you should be doing this, Rufus?

1052
01:09:34,230 --> 01:09:36,870
This isn't really for you or your dad.

1053
01:09:37,150 --> 01:09:38,870
It's to protect me.

1054
01:09:40,350 --> 01:09:43,630
I won't be safe until someone
takes down Listfield.

1055
01:09:44,510 --> 01:09:48,190
The keys are in the ignition.
If anything happens, just go.

1056
01:09:50,190 --> 01:09:52,630
I don't want to see you disbarred.

1057
01:09:55,350 --> 01:09:56,500
Rufus.

1058
01:10:03,990 --> 01:10:05,310
Good luck.

1059
01:10:26,470 --> 01:10:28,750
What are you doing here, miss?

1060
01:10:30,710 --> 01:10:34,390
Um, I was tired.
I just pulled over to have a rest.

1061
01:10:34,470 --> 01:10:36,460
You can't park here.
This is private property.

1062
01:10:36,550 --> 01:10:37,700
Sorry.

1063
01:11:00,270 --> 01:11:01,790
Rufus, it's me.

1064
01:11:02,990 --> 01:11:05,790
I posted your keys through the letterbox.

1065
01:11:06,990 --> 01:11:08,220
Call me.

1066
01:11:11,630 --> 01:11:15,750
- Who pays for your research work?
- I don't understand the question.

1067
01:11:17,190 --> 01:11:21,390
I'm funded by IRCP Oxford
and the Ford Institute in Washington.

1068
01:11:21,470 --> 01:11:24,510
Industry funds your projects there,
pays your salary?

1069
01:11:24,590 --> 01:11:28,380
Industry has the most interest
in protecting the environment.

1070
01:11:29,110 --> 01:11:31,990
Would you tell the court how much
you're being paid to give evidence?

1071
01:11:32,070 --> 01:11:33,350
How does it have any bearing?

1072
01:11:33,430 --> 01:11:37,430
Professor, you've been asked
a legitimate question.

1073
01:11:39,550 --> 01:11:40,990
It's £90,000.

1074
01:11:42,270 --> 01:11:43,750
For what exactly?

1075
01:11:43,830 --> 01:11:47,270
My expertise, my time, my
research into background.

1076
01:11:48,790 --> 01:11:50,110
Right, okay.

1077
01:11:50,870 --> 01:11:54,710
- Who's writing the cheque?
- The company who hired me, Mentor.

1078
01:11:54,790 --> 01:11:57,230
They're a problem-solving logistics company.

1079
01:11:57,310 --> 01:11:59,070
And they're funded by industry?

1080
01:11:59,150 --> 01:12:02,590
The industry is concerned to be well regulated.

1081
01:12:03,150 --> 01:12:05,950
In the '70s and '80s, there were a lot of

1082
01:12:06,630 --> 01:12:10,070
alternative, green-orientated
epidemiological studies.

1083
01:12:10,150 --> 01:12:12,380
Most were inaccurate.

1084
01:12:12,470 --> 01:12:15,990
The methodology involved small,
community-based studies.

1085
01:12:16,150 --> 01:12:19,510
Have you put forward the view
that the symptoms of people

1086
01:12:19,590 --> 01:12:22,790
in the immediate vicinity of sites like Peaks Farm

1087
01:12:23,390 --> 01:12:27,180
are produced by hysteria? Are psychosomatic?

1088
01:12:27,750 --> 01:12:28,900
It often happens.

1089
01:12:28,990 --> 01:12:30,980
Like Mr and Mrs Peacock's daughter, Henri?

1090
01:12:31,070 --> 01:12:33,380
Or the woman two miles away
whose baby was mutagenic

1091
01:12:33,470 --> 01:12:37,100
or the other 12 similar babies
born within a 10-mile radius

1092
01:12:37,190 --> 01:12:40,870
of the incinerator?
- They are a statistical aberration.

1093
01:12:41,190 --> 01:12:45,470
Our studies involving over-25,000
people are more accurate.

1094
01:12:45,550 --> 01:12:49,710
Bought and paid for by the industry.
You delivered what they paid for.

1095
01:12:50,270 --> 01:12:53,630
I think you've identified
too much with your clients.

1096
01:12:55,030 --> 01:12:58,990
His studies only look at
males, not pregnant women.

1097
01:13:03,510 --> 01:13:06,980
Isn't it true, Professor, that in your study
you look only at males?

1098
01:13:07,070 --> 01:13:10,070
We don't get to see the effect
on female reproduction.

1099
01:13:11,870 --> 01:13:14,230
There are more males in this particular industry.

1100
01:13:14,310 --> 01:13:17,510
But you chose not to include their female partners

1101
01:13:17,670 --> 01:13:19,740
or pregnancies and births.

1102
01:13:20,550 --> 01:13:21,670
Hmm?

1103
01:13:22,390 --> 01:13:26,830
Is there no value in small,
community-based studies?

1104
01:13:26,910 --> 01:13:28,870
It would be foolish to say none.

1105
01:13:28,950 --> 01:13:32,310
They certainly reflect how people feel when asked.

1106
01:13:32,390 --> 01:13:35,350
They are irrelevant to science.

1107
01:13:35,430 --> 01:13:39,140
If you take a small group who are
unhappy about a particular industry,

1108
01:13:39,230 --> 01:13:42,430
they will tend to blame that industry for all
of their problems.

1109
01:13:42,510 --> 01:13:44,580
Thank you, Professor Neame.

1110
01:13:46,270 --> 01:13:49,660
Professor Pao, before we leave the matter of
epidemiological studies,

1111
01:13:49,750 --> 01:13:53,430
would you mind if I asked you
a few more questions, please?

1112
01:13:54,950 --> 01:13:57,990
Thank you, Professor Neame. You can step down.

1113
01:14:07,350 --> 01:14:09,340
You are still under oath.

1114
01:14:09,950 --> 01:14:13,390
Professor, is there significant
difference in accuracy

1115
01:14:13,470 --> 01:14:17,070
between large epidemiological
studies and small ones?

1116
01:14:17,430 --> 01:14:18,500
Yes.

1117
01:14:18,790 --> 01:14:23,660
Small-scale qualitative studies stay focused
on their original objective.

1118
01:14:24,430 --> 01:14:28,190
With large scale reviews, multi-centred studies,

1119
01:14:28,270 --> 01:14:30,830
you are dealing with very diffused statistics

1120
01:14:30,910 --> 01:14:32,900
from which it is impossible to extrapolate

1121
01:14:32,990 --> 01:14:37,460
specific and exact information about sub-groups,
like pregnant women.

1122
01:14:38,710 --> 01:14:42,630
Big studies give more opportunities to disguise
some results.

1123
01:14:43,110 --> 01:14:46,150
Are you saying researchers
intentionally disguise the results?

1124
01:14:46,630 --> 01:14:48,390
Few of us are without bias.

1125
01:14:52,710 --> 01:14:55,470
Thank you, Professor. You can step down.

1126
01:15:00,350 --> 01:15:03,350
Mrs Peacock, is your daughter in the building?

1127
01:15:03,630 --> 01:15:05,860
- Yes.
- Might I see her?

1128
01:15:06,990 --> 01:15:08,220
Yeah.

1129
01:15:43,350 --> 01:15:45,260
She has a lovely smile.

1130
01:15:54,350 --> 01:15:56,340
Are you all right, Judge?

1131
01:15:58,430 --> 01:16:02,470
That baby seemed so valiant

1132
01:16:04,070 --> 01:16:05,870
against all the odds.

1133
01:16:06,190 --> 01:16:08,830
I hope you find in the parents' favour.

1134
01:16:14,670 --> 01:16:17,350
Find Mrs Mills for me, would you, Coop?

1135
01:16:26,870 --> 01:16:29,710
Mrs Mills, the Judge would like to see you.

1136
01:16:31,550 --> 01:16:32,620
See you.

1137
01:16:39,190 --> 01:16:41,950
- Mrs Mills, Judge.
- Thanks, Coop.

1138
01:16:48,310 --> 01:16:50,830
This was a very difficult case to resolve.

1139
01:16:52,670 --> 01:16:55,470
I feel such a burden of
responsibility to that child.

1140
01:16:55,550 --> 01:16:58,310
Good. Then find in her favour.

1141
01:16:59,030 --> 01:17:02,820
There is no doubt that dioxins
caused these horrific deformities.

1142
01:17:03,830 --> 01:17:05,990
What did Cardinal imagine was
happening to the dioxins

1143
01:17:05,995 --> 01:17:08,795
when they went up their chimneys?
- Jo.

1144
01:17:10,950 --> 01:17:14,420
You have to have shown that Cardinal was the cause

1145
01:17:15,350 --> 01:17:17,820
and they foresaw it happening.

1146
01:17:19,070 --> 01:17:21,910
You haven't made your case on either point.

1147
01:17:26,150 --> 01:17:29,270
Thank you for telling me ahead of the judgment.

1148
01:17:29,910 --> 01:17:34,110
I'd have liked to have helped this child,
but the law won't let me.

1149
01:17:35,470 --> 01:17:37,430
- I'll warn the Peacocks.
- Okay.

1150
01:17:37,510 --> 01:17:42,460
By the way, there is no evidence against Haughton.

1151
01:17:44,830 --> 01:17:47,710
Tomorrow, Parliament will debate my removal.

1152
01:17:48,150 --> 01:17:50,431
- They can't throw you off the bench.
- Oh, yes, they can.

1153
01:17:50,436 --> 01:17:53,156
- Well, then stop them...
- What would you have me do?

1154
01:17:55,110 --> 01:17:57,500
Argue your case some more
with the Attorney General.

1155
01:17:57,590 --> 01:17:58,630
Jo.

1156
01:17:59,190 --> 01:18:01,750
I've got nothing left to fight them with.

1157
01:18:01,830 --> 01:18:04,390
John, you cannot give up.

1158
01:18:04,470 --> 01:18:08,830
If you are not on the bench,
there will be no one left to resist Haughton.

1159
01:18:08,910 --> 01:18:11,140
None of the other judges will.

1160
01:18:25,230 --> 01:18:29,470
Unless my impeachment is stopped,
I'll take the diaries to the media.

1161
01:18:30,350 --> 01:18:33,740
That's below the belt, John.
- These are unfounded allegations.

1162
01:18:33,830 --> 01:18:35,740
It's a trade-off, Alan.

1163
01:18:35,870 --> 01:18:39,230
Ugly, but you get the
diaries, I stay on the bench.

1164
01:18:40,430 --> 01:18:41,870
And Cardinal?

1165
01:18:46,790 --> 01:18:49,590
- I think you're bluffing.
- Well...

1166
01:18:51,510 --> 01:18:52,740
Call me.

1167
01:19:04,990 --> 01:19:09,380
I can't stop your removal on my own.
I'll have to talk to my colleagues.

1168
01:19:11,150 --> 01:19:12,750
And the diaries?

1169
01:19:26,750 --> 01:19:29,220
How near to ending is the Cardinal case, Ian?

1170
01:19:29,310 --> 01:19:32,540
- Quite close.
- If we delay for his judgment,

1171
01:19:32,630 --> 01:19:36,310
there won't be enough time for
both Houses to petition the Queen.

1172
01:19:36,390 --> 01:19:39,830
We can't trust him to deal
with this in the right way.

1173
01:19:40,030 --> 01:19:42,150
We must remove him now.

1174
01:19:42,230 --> 01:19:46,550
But if he does go the right way, we'll
have finally brought him to heel.

1175
01:19:46,630 --> 01:19:50,550
You know the political maxim, Neil,
"If in doubt, delay."

1176
01:19:51,790 --> 01:19:54,350
I am going to advise the PM to wait.

1177
01:19:54,550 --> 01:19:56,830
I'm going to advise him not to.

1178
01:20:00,390 --> 01:20:02,780
Jo, this came from Rufus Barron.

1179
01:20:02,870 --> 01:20:05,830
It's over, Charlie, for us, your Dad, too.

1180
01:20:06,550 --> 01:20:08,270
I've got to talk to the Peacocks.

1181
01:20:08,350 --> 01:20:11,580
Cardinal knew that there was a
design fault in their chimneys.

1182
01:20:11,670 --> 01:20:13,110
What?

1183
01:20:13,190 --> 01:20:16,710
Mentor advised them it would be cheaper to settle
any possible claims

1184
01:20:16,790 --> 01:20:19,630
that might arise than rectify this problem.

1185
01:20:20,590 --> 01:20:23,390
Are we imagining this?
Did you talk to Mr Barron?

1186
01:20:23,470 --> 01:20:25,300
I can't get hold of him, but it's real.

1187
01:20:25,390 --> 01:20:27,910
We've got to talk to the Judge.
See if he'll admit it. He must!

1188
01:20:27,915 --> 01:20:29,145
Come on.

1189
01:20:31,390 --> 01:20:34,780
Alan, tell Haughton and
Rochester all bets are off.

1190
01:20:40,590 --> 01:20:44,910
It's too late. Read my lips.
It is too late to submit such evidence.

1191
01:20:44,990 --> 01:20:50,700
Mrs Channing, I have heard your argument for not
admitting this evidence.

1192
01:20:50,910 --> 01:20:55,670
I have been directed to the law
pertaining to late evidence.

1193
01:20:55,750 --> 01:20:58,670
Cases of this nature, where lives are at risk,

1194
01:20:58,750 --> 01:21:02,190
where the possible future
of the species is at risk,

1195
01:21:02,270 --> 01:21:06,470
are not a simply a matter of winning
or losing, but of finding the truth.

1196
01:21:06,550 --> 01:21:10,510
- Which you know, of course.
- We will call the author of this report.

1197
01:21:10,590 --> 01:21:14,870
I will ask you to recall Sir
Peter Tufton of Cardinal PLC

1198
01:21:14,950 --> 01:21:18,870
for whom this report was commissioned.
- I'm not inclined to do that.

1199
01:21:18,950 --> 01:21:22,260
I will have the truth here.

1200
01:21:25,030 --> 01:21:28,660
Sir Peter, will you go back
into the witness box, please?

1201
01:21:44,510 --> 01:21:47,510
- Remember you are still under oath.
- My lord.

1202
01:21:47,590 --> 01:21:50,350
Will you look at this report? Thank you.

1203
01:21:51,110 --> 01:21:54,710
Made on behalf of Cardinal PLC by Mentor

1204
01:21:54,790 --> 01:21:57,350
as to the state of the incinerator chimney.

1205
01:22:05,790 --> 01:22:08,790
Will you tell the court when you first saw it?

1206
01:22:16,590 --> 01:22:18,390
May 1999.

1207
01:22:20,550 --> 01:22:22,620
One year after the plant opened.

1208
01:22:28,430 --> 01:22:30,790
He's allowed in late evidence.

1209
01:22:30,870 --> 01:22:32,870
The question is what are you going to do about it?

1210
01:22:32,950 --> 01:22:35,590
It's too late for impeachment.
Parliament has prorogued.

1211
01:22:35,670 --> 01:22:39,380
- I want Deed removed.
- Only the judges can stop him now.

1212
01:22:41,550 --> 01:22:45,100
We must proceed. The judges
must insist on his removal.

1213
01:22:45,190 --> 01:22:47,660
The judges accept that he behaved shamefully,

1214
01:22:47,750 --> 01:22:49,660
but they are unanimous in this,

1215
01:22:49,750 --> 01:22:52,710
that to pursue Deed now would seem as if

1216
01:22:52,790 --> 01:22:55,830
they're paying him back for pursuing you.

1217
01:22:55,910 --> 01:22:57,550
That has no bearing, Joe.

1218
01:22:57,630 --> 01:23:01,830
Joe never said it had, only that
that is how it will appear.

1219
01:23:04,750 --> 01:23:08,220
- What do you say, Michael?
- Oh, I'd like Deed stopped.

1220
01:23:08,590 --> 01:23:11,550
This looked like the means, but it's not to be.

1221
01:23:11,630 --> 01:23:15,150
- We could recall Parliament.
- I would advise against it.

1222
01:23:15,390 --> 01:23:19,990
Impeachment would never be achieved on a vote
in these circumstances.

1223
01:23:21,030 --> 01:23:24,390
No. I won't let this happen.

1224
01:23:27,110 --> 01:23:31,580
You have to be philosophical about these things,
Neil, like me.

1225
01:23:33,630 --> 01:23:36,190
It's not you who will lose his job.

1226
01:23:36,270 --> 01:23:38,870
I'm the other half of the accusation.

1227
01:23:39,590 --> 01:23:42,030
Only there won't be any more evidence, Neil.

1228
01:23:42,110 --> 01:23:45,260
- Are you sure of that?
- Very sure.

1229
01:24:13,750 --> 01:24:16,190
You each have a copy of my full judgment,

1230
01:24:16,270 --> 01:24:18,710
which I will now give to you in summary.

1231
01:24:19,710 --> 01:24:23,420
This was a very difficult judgment to arrive at.

1232
01:24:24,230 --> 01:24:27,460
On the one hand, we must try to protect
the individual and the community

1233
01:24:27,550 --> 01:24:31,100
from the effects of potentially
polluting industry.

1234
01:24:31,190 --> 01:24:35,660
On the other hand, we must recognise
industry's place in modern society.

1235
01:24:36,190 --> 01:24:39,470
Industry is a means of providing both employment

1236
01:24:39,550 --> 01:24:41,860
and the products that most people want.

1237
01:24:41,950 --> 01:24:46,230
Or in the case of Cardinal Waste Management PLC,

1238
01:24:46,310 --> 01:24:49,030
the means of disposing of the waste.

1239
01:24:49,710 --> 01:24:51,700
Therein lies the problem.

1240
01:24:52,990 --> 01:24:57,460
The most difficult part of the case involving
Mr and Mrs Peacock

1241
01:24:57,550 --> 01:24:59,540
and their daughter, Henri, and Cardinal,

1242
01:24:59,630 --> 01:25:02,910
concerned two essential points to be proven.

1243
01:25:02,990 --> 01:25:06,110
Causation and foreseeability.

1244
01:25:06,190 --> 01:25:10,190
It was shown that dioxins caused the problem.

1245
01:25:10,270 --> 01:25:13,420
But was it brought to their doorstep by Cardinal?

1246
01:25:14,830 --> 01:25:19,990
Well, from the report commissioned
by Cardinal from Mentor,

1247
01:25:20,550 --> 01:25:24,940
it's absolutely clear that Cardinal knew that
there was a problem

1248
01:25:25,030 --> 01:25:27,990
with the incinerator plant at Peaks Farm

1249
01:25:28,070 --> 01:25:31,190
and that this problem was due to a design fault.

1250
01:25:32,030 --> 01:25:35,710
Yet knowing this they chose, on economic grounds,

1251
01:25:35,790 --> 01:25:39,340
to bear the cost of any claims made against them,

1252
01:25:39,430 --> 01:25:43,350
cynically stating that any damages
awarded in a British court

1253
01:25:43,910 --> 01:25:48,540
would cost them far less than shutting down
and redesigning the plant.

1254
01:25:50,990 --> 01:25:56,390
So, in such a situation, the
law allows for exemplary

1255
01:25:56,470 --> 01:25:59,030
or punitive damages to be awarded.

1256
01:25:59,910 --> 01:26:03,670
I'm therefore awarding damages
towards the care of baby Henri

1257
01:26:03,750 --> 01:26:07,870
of £105,000 per annum for life.

1258
01:26:08,630 --> 01:26:14,820
I'm awarding to Mr and Mrs Peacock
£500,000 for the loss of their home.

1259
01:26:15,950 --> 01:26:20,500
I'm also awarding damages to
Paul and Pauline Peacock,

1260
01:26:21,350 --> 01:26:25,470
of £3,500,000

1261
01:26:25,590 --> 01:26:27,980
for the distress this
teratogenic birth caused them.

1262
01:26:28,070 --> 01:26:30,350
These are my principal rulings.

1263
01:26:31,750 --> 01:26:36,070
In the meanwhile, I am sending a report to the DPP

1264
01:26:36,150 --> 01:26:39,940
and I'm asking him to explore the possibility
of bringing charges

1265
01:26:40,030 --> 01:26:44,950
against both Cardinal and
Mentor in respect of injuries

1266
01:26:45,030 --> 01:26:51,110
caused to Mr and Mrs Peacock, baby Henri and other
members of the community.

1267
01:26:53,390 --> 01:26:54,990
All rise.

1268
01:26:58,110 --> 01:26:59,430
We will appeal.

1269
01:26:59,510 --> 01:27:03,430
George, the world is at last waking up to what
we're all doing.

1270
01:27:10,350 --> 01:27:11,420
Dad?

1271
01:27:15,550 --> 01:27:17,030
Rufus is dead.

1272
01:27:19,510 --> 01:27:21,900
The police said there was a note.

1273
01:27:23,270 --> 01:27:24,870
They killed him,

1274
01:27:26,670 --> 01:27:28,870
just like he knew they would.

